/* Economists and ideas; Why did this happen? Generated; do not edit by hand. */
window.__IDEAS__={"version":"ideas-2026.10.2","economists":[{"id":"smith","name":"Adam Smith","life":"1723–1790","era":"Classical political economy","headline":"Smith showed how an economy coordinated by prices, competition and the division of labour could generate rising output without central direction, while arguing that this order rested on law, institutions and moral norms.","context":"Smith was a moral philosopher in Enlightenment Scotland, teaching at the University of Glasgow. Britain’s trade policy at the time was shaped by mercantilist thinking: the view that national wealth meant accumulating gold and silver, so exports should be encouraged and imports restricted. Chartered companies held monopoly privileges, and guilds and apprenticeship rules limited who could work in many trades. Smith asked a different question. What actually makes a nation richer, and why do some economies grow while others stagnate? His answer moved attention away from stocks of precious metal and towards the productive capacity of labour, the extent of markets and the rules that govern exchange.","ideas":[{"h":"The division of labour","p":"Smith’s opening example is a pin workshop. When production is split into many narrow tasks, each worker becomes more skilled, less time is lost switching between jobs, and simple tasks invite the invention of tools and machines. Output per worker can rise many times over. The limit on this specialisation is the size of the market: a small, isolated market cannot absorb the output of highly specialised producers, which is one reason Smith connected productivity growth to trade and transport."},{"h":"Self-interest and the price mechanism","p":"Smith argued that in competitive markets people pursuing their own gain are led to supply what others want. If a good is scarce, its market price rises above its ‘natural price’, drawing in resources until supply catches up. This is an early account of how relative prices allocate resources. The phrase ‘invisible hand’ appears only once in The Wealth of Nations, in a passage about investment at home, and was not the slogan for unregulated markets it later became."},{"h":"A critique of mercantilism and monopoly","p":"Smith treated restrictions on trade as a way for organised merchants and manufacturers to gain at consumers’ expense. Tariffs, bounties and monopoly charters raised prices and diverted capital into less productive uses. He was openly suspicious of business interests seeking political favours, and warned that producers in the same trade tend to look for ways to raise prices. His argument for open markets was therefore partly an argument against concentrated private power, not simply for business."},{"h":"Moral sentiments and the role of the state","p":"In The Theory of Moral Sentiments, Smith explained social behaviour through sympathy: our ability to imagine others’ situations and to judge ourselves as an impartial spectator might. Markets, in this view, depend on trust, justice and shared norms. Smith also gave government real tasks: defence, a system of justice, public works and institutions that private firms would not provide profitably, and support for the basic education of ordinary workers, whose minds he feared repetitive work could dull."}],"contribution":"Smith gave economics its first systematic account of how a market economy works as a whole. He linked productivity to specialisation, explained how market prices tend to move towards costs of production, and treated the growth of national income, rather than the accumulation of bullion, as the measure of a nation’s wealth. His method of combining abstract reasoning with historical and institutional detail set the agenda for the classical economists who followed, including Ricardo and Malthus.","assumptions":["Individuals are largely motivated by self-interest, but within a framework of moral norms and law","Competition is sufficiently strong that no single seller can control price for long","Secure property rights and the enforcement of contracts","Labour and capital can move between uses in response to price signals"],"influence":"The Wealth of Nations shaped nineteenth-century British debates on free trade and helped discredit mercantilist policy. Classical economists built directly on his theories of value, distribution and growth. Later, Smith became a symbol in arguments for market liberalisation, sometimes in a narrower form than his writing supports. Historians of economic thought and development economists have recently drawn more on his institutional arguments, his concern with the conditions of the poor and his scepticism of monopoly.","criticisms":["His theory of value moves between labour-cost and cost-of-production explanations without fully reconciling them, a gap later economists tried to resolve.","The account of self-regulating markets says relatively little about externalities, public goods beyond a short list, or prolonged unemployment, which later analysis made central.","Some historians argue that his story of growth underplays the role of state support, protection and coercion, including colonial trade and slavery, in Britain’s own industrialisation."],"modern":"Questions Smith raised still organise much of economics. Debates about global supply chains are debates about the division of labour and the extent of the market. Competition authorities share his worry that firms may collude or lobby for protection. Behavioural and institutional economists cite The Theory of Moral Sentiments when arguing that trust and norms support exchange. Reading both of his books together is a useful corrective to treating him as a one-line defender of laissez-faire. In 4.1, absolute and comparative advantage are HL only.","works":[{"t":"The Theory of Moral Sentiments","y":1759},{"t":"An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations","y":1776}],"honours":null,"misread":"Students often treat Smith as arguing that government should do nothing and that self-interest is all that matters. In fact he gave the state significant duties, criticised merchants who sought monopoly, and placed markets inside a wider moral and legal framework.","subs":["1.1","2.3","4.1"],"kc":["Choice","Efficiency","Interdependence"],"terms":["Price mechanism","Free market economy","Absolute advantage","Property rights","Mixed economy"],"dg":["ds"],"question":"To what extent does Smith’s argument about the ‘extent of the market’ help to explain why small, landlocked economies may find it harder to specialise?","connects":[{"id":"ricardo","why":"Ricardo extended Smith’s case for trade from absolute to comparative advantage."},{"id":"hayek","why":"Hayek developed Smith’s idea of an unplanned market order into an argument about knowledge and prices."},{"id":"marx","why":"Marx read Smith closely and reworked the classical labour theory of value into a critique of capitalism."}]},{"id":"ricardo","name":"David Ricardo","life":"1772–1823","era":"Classical political economy","headline":"Ricardo showed that two countries can both gain from trade even when one is more productive at everything, and built abstract models of distribution that shaped how economists reason.","context":"Ricardo made a fortune as a stockbroker and financier in London before turning to economics and later sitting in Parliament. He wrote during and after the Napoleonic Wars, when Britain’s Corn Laws restricted grain imports to protect landowners’ incomes. Food prices were high, population was growing and more marginal land was being farmed. Ricardo wanted to know how the national product is divided between landlords, capitalists and workers, and what happens to profits and growth when cheap foreign grain is kept out. His answers were framed as deliberately simplified models, a method that became characteristic of economics.","ideas":[{"h":"Comparative advantage","p":"Ricardo’s example used England and Portugal producing cloth and wine. Even if Portugal needed less labour for both goods, each country gains by specialising in the good it produces at a lower opportunity cost and trading for the other. The gain comes from differences in relative efficiency, not absolute efficiency. This idea, which is HL only in the IB course (4.1), separates the case for trade from the question of which country is more productive overall."},{"h":"Differential rent and diminishing returns","p":"As population grows, farming extends to less fertile land, so each extra unit of food costs more labour to produce. Rent, in Ricardo’s account, arises because better land yields a surplus over the worst land in use. Landlords receive that surplus without adding to output. The idea is an early form of the law of diminishing marginal returns (HL only in the IB course, 2.2) and of thinking ‘at the margin’, where the last unit of land determines price."},{"h":"Distribution, profits and the Corn Laws","p":"Ricardo argued that rising food costs push up the wages needed to keep workers alive, which squeezes profits. Lower profits reduce the incentive to accumulate capital, slowing growth towards a ‘stationary state’. Restricting grain imports accelerates this process by forcing cultivation onto poorer land. Free trade in corn, by contrast, would lower food prices, protect profits and support investment, at the expense of landlords’ rents."},{"h":"Abstract modelling","p":"Ricardo reasoned from a few stylised assumptions to strong conclusions, isolating one mechanism at a time. This approach gave clarity and allowed general results, such as comparative advantage, that hold within the model. It also carried a risk later called the ‘Ricardian vice’: applying conclusions from a highly simplified model directly to policy without checking whether the assumptions fit the real case."}],"contribution":"Comparative advantage remains one of the foundations of international economics and one of the least intuitive results in the subject. Ricardo’s rent theory introduced reasoning at the margin, and his model of how income is shared among social classes made distribution a central question. His method of building a simplified model to isolate one mechanism set the pattern for much later economic theory, for better and for worse.","assumptions":["Labour is the only factor of production, and its productivity differs between countries","Factors move freely within a country but not between countries","Constant opportunity costs and no transport costs","Full employment, so specialisation reallocates rather than idles resources"],"influence":"The Corn Laws were repealed in 1846, and Ricardo’s arguments were part of the long debate leading there. His trade theory became the starting point for later models, including those that explain comparative advantage through differences in factor endowments. Marx built on his labour theory of value, while the marginalist economists of the 1870s replaced it. Modern trade agreements and debates about who loses from trade still rely on Ricardian reasoning.","criticisms":["The single-factor model hides the distributional effects of trade: some workers and regions can lose even when the country gains overall.","Comparative advantage is treated as fixed, whereas later economists argued it can be created through learning, investment and policy, which underpins the infant industry argument.","The model assumes full employment; if displaced workers do not find new jobs, the predicted gains may not appear in the short run.","His predicted fall in profits towards a stationary state did not occur, partly because technical change in agriculture offset diminishing returns."],"modern":"Ricardo’s logic is used whenever economists explain why rich and poor countries both trade, or why outsourcing a task can make sense even for a firm that could do it more efficiently itself. Debates about the regional effects of import competition, about reshoring production and about industrial policy are, in part, arguments about the limits of his assumptions. Students meet his model in its simplest linear form, but the reasoning about opportunity cost carries over to richer settings.","works":[{"t":"On the Principles of Political Economy and Taxation","y":1817}],"honours":null,"misread":"A common misreading is that a country should import only goods it cannot produce at all, or that the most efficient country gains nothing from trade. Comparative advantage depends on relative opportunity costs, so both countries can gain even when one has an absolute advantage in every good.","subs":["4.1","4.3","1.1"],"kc":["Interdependence","Efficiency","Choice"],"terms":["Comparative advantage","Absolute advantage","Opportunity cost","Law of diminishing marginal returns","Constant opportunity cost"],"dg":["compadv","ppc"],"question":"How far do the assumptions of Ricardo’s two-country model limit its usefulness in explaining a present-day trade dispute you have studied?","connects":[{"id":"smith","why":"Ricardo refined Smith’s absolute-advantage argument for trade."},{"id":"malthus","why":"Ricardo and Malthus corresponded at length, disagreeing on the Corn Laws and on general gluts."},{"id":"marx","why":"Marx adapted Ricardo’s labour theory of value and his class-based theory of distribution."}]},{"id":"malthus","name":"Thomas Robert Malthus","life":"1766–1834","era":"Classical political economy","headline":"Malthus made the relationship between population, resources and living standards a central economic question, and raised early doubts about whether total demand would be sufficient to keep production growing.","context":"Malthus was an English clergyman who later taught political economy. He wrote in the 1790s, when thinkers such as William Godwin and the Marquis de Condorcet argued that human society could progress towards equality and abundance. Malthus was sceptical. England’s population was rising, poor relief under the Poor Laws was a matter of public argument, and food supply depended on limited land. He set out to show that any improvement in living standards would tend to be absorbed by population growth, unless something restrained births. The argument was as much a contribution to political debate as to economic theory.","ideas":[{"h":"The principle of population","p":"Malthus contrasted population, which he argued tends to grow geometrically when unchecked, with food supply, which he argued grows at most arithmetically. If incomes rise, people marry earlier and have more surviving children, so population expands until food per person falls back. Living standards therefore gravitate towards subsistence. The mechanism relies on diminishing returns from a fixed supply of land (diminishing marginal returns is HL only in the IB course, 2.2)."},{"h":"Positive and preventive checks","p":"Population is held in line with food by two types of check. Positive checks raise the death rate: famine, disease and war. Preventive checks lower the birth rate, chiefly through later marriage, which Malthus called ‘moral restraint’. In later editions of the Essay he gave more weight to preventive checks, which softened his conclusions and allowed for the possibility of sustained, if modest, improvement."},{"h":"The Poor Laws","p":"Malthus argued that relief payments to the poor could raise births without raising food supply, pushing up food prices and leaving the poor no better off. This led him to oppose the existing system of poor relief. The argument was widely criticised at the time and since, both on empirical grounds and because of its harsh implications for the most vulnerable."},{"h":"Effective demand and gluts","p":"In his debates with Ricardo, Malthus questioned the view that supply creates its own demand. He argued that if landlords and capitalists saved too much and spent too little, a general glut of unsold goods could occur. Ricardo rejected this. Keynes later praised Malthus for raising the problem of insufficient demand, though Malthus did not develop a full theory of it."}],"contribution":"Malthus forced economists to consider natural limits and the feedback between incomes and population. His model is a clear early example of a dynamic system with an equilibrium that pulls living standards back towards a fixed level. It describes the pre-industrial world reasonably well, where gains in output tended to be absorbed by population growth. His questions about aggregate demand anticipated themes that Keynes developed more than a century later.","assumptions":["Land is fixed in supply and subject to diminishing returns","Higher incomes lead to higher fertility or lower mortality","Technical progress in food production is slow relative to population pressure","Restraint on births depends mainly on individual behaviour such as delayed marriage"],"influence":"Malthus influenced Ricardo’s theory of wages and rent, and Charles Darwin acknowledged that the Essay helped him think about competition for survival. In development economics the ‘Malthusian trap’ describes economies where income gains are absorbed by population growth, and growth economists use Malthusian models to describe the long period before industrialisation. His ideas also shaped later debates about famine, family planning and environmental limits.","criticisms":["Industrialisation and agricultural innovation allowed food output to outpace population growth for long periods, which his model did not anticipate.","Many countries experienced a demographic transition in which fertility fell as incomes rose, reversing the link he assumed.","Sen argued that famines are often caused by a collapse in people’s ability to obtain food, not by a simple shortage of food in total.","His case against poor relief was criticised as placing the costs of adjustment on the poorest people."],"modern":"Malthusian reasoning reappears in debates about whether finite natural resources and environmental limits constrain long-run growth. Critics reply that prices, substitution and innovation can relax resource constraints, as they did after his time. The question for students is not whether Malthus was simply right or wrong, but under what conditions resource pressure binds, and which institutions and technologies allow societies to escape it.","works":[{"t":"An Essay on the Principle of Population","y":1798}],"honours":null,"misread":"Students sometimes treat Malthus as having predicted that the world would run out of food by a particular date. His argument was about a tendency for living standards to be pulled back towards subsistence, and he revised it in later editions to give more weight to voluntary restraint on births.","subs":["4.9","1.1","4.7"],"kc":["Scarcity","Sustainability","Change"],"terms":["Scarcity","Law of diminishing marginal returns","Economic growth","Sustainable development","Poverty trap"],"dg":["ppc"],"question":"Under what conditions, if any, might Malthusian pressures help to explain slow growth in living standards in a low-income economy today?","connects":[{"id":"ricardo","why":"Their long correspondence shaped the classical theories of rent, wages and demand."},{"id":"keynes","why":"Keynes saw Malthus’s concern with gluts as a forerunner of his theory of effective demand."},{"id":"sen","why":"Sen’s entitlement analysis challenged food-supply explanations of famine."}]},{"id":"marx","name":"Karl Marx","life":"1818–1883","era":"Classical political economy and its critique","headline":"Marx analysed capitalism as a historically specific system driven by accumulation and conflict between classes, and asked how profit, inequality and instability arise from its normal workings.","context":"Marx was a German philosopher and journalist who spent most of his working life in exile in London. He wrote as industrial capitalism transformed Europe: factory employment expanded, cities grew quickly, working conditions were often harsh and periodic commercial crises threw people out of work. The revolutions of 1848 failed to deliver lasting political change. Marx studied the classical economists, especially Smith and Ricardo, and set out to explain where profit comes from, why capitalist economies grow and change so rapidly, and why they also seemed prone to crisis and to deep divisions between owners and workers.","ideas":[{"h":"Labour value and surplus value","p":"Building on Ricardo, Marx argued that the value of commodities reflects the labour time socially necessary to produce them. Workers sell their capacity to work for a wage that covers the cost of maintaining themselves. Because they can produce more value during the working day than that wage, the difference, which Marx called surplus value, is the source of profit. Profit, in this account, arises in production rather than from buying cheap and selling dear."},{"h":"Accumulation and competition","p":"Competition pushes capitalists to reinvest profits, mechanise and cut costs, or be driven out. Marx saw this as the source of capitalism’s exceptional dynamism and technical change. Mechanisation can also displace workers, creating what he called a reserve army of the unemployed that restrains wages. Over time, he argued, capital tends to become concentrated in fewer, larger firms."},{"h":"Crisis and instability","p":"Marx argued that capitalist economies are prone to recurring crises. Production is organised for profit, not directly for need, and decisions are made by many independent firms. Overproduction relative to what can be sold profitably, disproportions between sectors and pressures on the rate of profit can lead to downturns. These crises destroy some capital, which can then restore the conditions for renewed expansion."},{"h":"Historical change and class","p":"Marx treated economic systems as historical stages, each with its own relations of ownership and production. Capitalism, defined by private ownership of the means of production and wage labour, had replaced feudalism and, he expected, would itself be replaced. Conflict between classes over the division of output was, for him, a central driver of social and political change."}],"contribution":"Marx placed capital accumulation, technical change, the distribution of income between labour and capital, and the instability of growth at the centre of economic analysis. Many economists reject his value theory while still treating him as an important analyst of how capitalist economies evolve. His insistence that economic institutions are historically specific, rather than natural, also influenced economic history and sociology.","assumptions":["Value is determined by socially necessary labour time","Society is divided into classes defined by their relation to the means of production","Competition compels firms to accumulate and mechanise","Wages tend to be held near the cost of maintaining workers by the reserve army of labour"],"influence":"Marx’s ideas shaped labour movements and socialist and communist parties, and were cited to justify the planned economies of the twentieth century, whose design he did not set out in detail. Within economics, his work influenced theories of business cycles, development economics focused on structure and dependency, and the study of income distribution between wages and profits. Economists who reject his conclusions have still engaged with his questions about technological unemployment and concentration.","criticisms":["Eugen von Böhm-Bawerk and others argued that his labour theory of value could not consistently explain market prices, a difficulty known as the transformation problem.","His expectation of the growing impoverishment of workers was not borne out in many industrial economies, where real wages rose substantially over time.","The marginalist approach explained value through subjective preferences and scarcity, and most economists adopted it in place of labour value.","Economies that adopted central planning in his name faced serious problems of information and incentives, a point Mises and Hayek argued, though how far this tests Marx’s own analysis is debated."],"modern":"Marx is read today less as a guide to policy than as a source of questions. Debates about whether automation will reduce demand for labour, why the share of income going to labour changes over time, and why market power tends to concentrate echo themes he raised. In the IB course, his work is useful background for the basic economic questions, for comparing planned and free market economies, and for discussing inequality and the distribution of income.","works":[{"t":"The Communist Manifesto (with Friedrich Engels)","y":1848},{"t":"Capital, Volume I","y":1867}],"honours":null,"misread":"Students often equate Marx with the Soviet economy. Most of his economic writing is an analysis of capitalism, not a blueprint for a planned economy, and later regimes made many choices he did not describe.","subs":["1.1","3.4"],"kc":["Equity","Change","Interdependence"],"terms":["Planned economy","Free market economy","Basic economic questions","Factors of production","Equality","Wealth"],"dg":["lorenz"],"question":"To what extent do concerns about automation and the labour share of income resemble the questions Marx asked about mechanisation and the reserve army of labour?","connects":[{"id":"ricardo","why":"Marx took Ricardo’s labour theory of value and class analysis as his starting point."},{"id":"hayek","why":"Hayek’s knowledge argument was a central objection to economies planned in Marx’s name."},{"id":"robinson","why":"Joan Robinson wrote a sympathetic but critical study of Marx’s economics."}]},{"id":"marshall","name":"Alfred Marshall","life":"1842–1924","era":"Neoclassical economics","headline":"Marshall combined demand, built on marginal utility, with supply, built on costs of production, into the partial-equilibrium framework of supply and demand that most introductory economics still uses.","context":"Marshall trained in mathematics and moral philosophy and spent most of his career at Cambridge. In the 1870s economists such as Jevons, Menger and Walras had argued that value depends on marginal utility, challenging the classical emphasis on costs of production. The debate risked splitting the subject. Marshall, writing in Victorian Britain amid concern about urban poverty, wanted economics to be both rigorous and useful to policy. He sought a framework that reconciled the two views of value, kept mathematics in the background and could be applied to the study of real industries and the condition of workers.","ideas":[{"h":"The scissors of supply and demand","p":"Marshall compared the question of whether utility or cost of production determines value to asking which blade of a pair of scissors does the cutting. Price is set by demand and supply together. Demand reflects consumers’ marginal valuation, while supply reflects producers’ marginal cost. Earlier writers such as Cournot and Fleeming Jenkin had drawn demand and supply curves, but Marshall’s version, with price on the vertical axis and an equilibrium where the curves cross, is the one that became standard."},{"h":"Partial equilibrium and ceteris paribus","p":"Rather than model the whole economy at once, Marshall analysed one market at a time, holding other influences constant. This ‘other things being equal’ method makes it possible to trace the effect of a single change, such as a tax or a shift in demand. Its limit is that effects in one market can spill back into others, so the method works best for markets that are small relative to the economy."},{"h":"Time periods and elasticity","p":"Marshall distinguished the market period, short run and long run by how far producers can adjust inputs. Supply becomes more responsive as time passes. He also gave a precise form to the price elasticity of demand: the responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price. Elasticity let economists predict, for example, how the burden of a tax would be shared between buyers and sellers."},{"h":"Consumer surplus and external economies","p":"Consumer surplus measures the gap between what buyers would be willing to pay and what they actually pay, giving a money measure of welfare. Marshall also described external economies: cost advantages a firm gains from the growth of its whole industry or district, such as a pool of skilled labour and specialised suppliers. Both ideas later became tools for analysing welfare and the location of industry."}],"contribution":"Marshall built the toolkit that most microeconomics courses still start with: supply and demand curves, equilibrium, elasticity, consumer and producer surplus, and the short-run and long-run distinction. He established economics as a separate university discipline at Cambridge and trained a generation that included Pigou and Keynes. His approach made it possible to analyse real policy questions, such as the incidence of taxes, with clear and teachable diagrams. In the IB course, diminishing marginal utility as an assumption behind demand is HL only.","assumptions":["Other things are held equal while one variable changes","Consumers experience diminishing marginal utility from additional units","Firms respond to price by adjusting output towards the point where price covers marginal cost","Markets are competitive enough that individual buyers and sellers are price takers"],"influence":"Marshall’s Principles was the leading economics textbook in the English-speaking world for decades. Pigou built welfare economics on his concepts of consumer surplus and external effects, while Robinson and others reworked his theory of the firm. His ideas on industrial districts were revived in the study of clusters and agglomeration. Standard diagrams for taxes, subsidies, price controls and elasticity descend directly from his work.","criticisms":["Partial-equilibrium analysis can miss feedback between markets, which general-equilibrium approaches such as that of Walras try to capture.","His treatment of increasing returns sits uneasily with perfect competition, a tension that Piero Sraffa and later Joan Robinson pressed in the 1920s and 1930s.","Measuring welfare by consumer surplus assumes a constant marginal utility of money, which is unlikely to hold for large changes or across people with very different incomes."],"modern":"Every time a student draws a demand and supply diagram, calculates price elasticity or shades consumer surplus, they are using Marshall’s tools. Competition authorities, tax analysts and regulators still use partial-equilibrium models for practical decisions. Economic geographers draw on his external economies to explain why firms in some industries cluster in particular cities. The key skill is knowing when holding other things constant is a reasonable simplification.","works":[{"t":"Principles of Economics","y":1890}],"honours":null,"misread":"Students sometimes think a change in price ‘shifts’ the demand curve. In Marshall’s framework a change in the good’s own price causes a movement along the curve; only changes in other conditions, held constant under ceteris paribus, shift it.","subs":["2.1","2.3","2.5"],"kc":["Choice","Efficiency","Change"],"terms":["Ceteris paribus","Market equilibrium","Price elasticity of demand","Consumer surplus","Law of diminishing marginal utility","Movement along the demand curve"],"dg":["ds","ped"],"question":"How far does holding ‘other things equal’ limit the usefulness of a demand and supply diagram for analysing a large change, such as a sharp rise in the price of energy?","connects":[{"id":"pigou","why":"Pigou succeeded Marshall at Cambridge and developed welfare economics from his ideas."},{"id":"keynes","why":"Keynes was Marshall’s student before breaking with the Cambridge tradition on aggregate demand."},{"id":"robinson","why":"Robinson rebuilt Marshall’s theory of the firm for imperfectly competitive markets."}]},{"id":"pigou","name":"Arthur Cecil Pigou","life":"1877–1959","era":"Neoclassical welfare economics","headline":"Pigou showed how private and social costs and benefits can diverge, and argued that taxes and subsidies could bring market outcomes closer to what is best for society.","context":"Pigou succeeded Marshall as professor of political economy at Cambridge. He wrote in the early twentieth century, when industrial pollution, poor urban housing and dangerous working conditions were prominent public concerns, and when the scope of government in Britain was widening. Marshall had pointed to effects that spill beyond a firm. Pigou set out to build a systematic welfare economics: a way of judging when market outcomes maximise the economic welfare of society, and when there is a case for the state to intervene to correct them.","ideas":[{"h":"Private versus social net product","p":"Pigou distinguished the private return from an activity, which the decision-maker considers, from its social return, which includes effects on others. When a factory’s smoke damages laundry and health nearby, the social cost exceeds the private cost. Because the firm ignores these costs, it produces more than is socially optimal. In modern terms this is a negative externality of production, where marginal social cost exceeds marginal private cost."},{"h":"Corrective taxes and subsidies","p":"If the divergence can be measured, the state can impose a tax equal to the external cost at the optimal output, so that the firm faces the full social cost. Where activities generate external benefits, such as education or planting trees, a subsidy can encourage output towards the social optimum. This is now known as a Pigouvian tax or subsidy, and it underpins modern carbon taxes."},{"h":"Price discrimination","p":"Pigou classified price discrimination into first, second and third degrees. A seller may charge each buyer their maximum willingness to pay, charge different prices according to quantity bought, or charge different prices to separate groups of buyers. His analysis showed that the welfare effects depend on whether discrimination raises total output, not simply on whether some buyers pay more."},{"h":"Welfare and distribution","p":"Pigou argued that, other things equal, transferring income from richer to poorer people tends to raise total welfare, because an extra pound is likely to matter more to someone with less (an application of diminishing marginal utility, which is HL only in the IB course). He paired this with concern about the effect of transfers on incentives to produce. The argument linked welfare economics to debates about progressive taxation and social policy."}],"contribution":"Pigou gave economists a framework for judging market outcomes against a social standard and for designing policy to correct market failure. The distinction between private and social cost, and the idea of internalising an externality through price, remain central in environmental, health and urban economics. His classification of price discrimination is still taught. More broadly, he helped establish welfare economics as a field in which efficiency and distribution are analysed together.","assumptions":["External costs and benefits can be identified and measured in money terms","Government has the information and motivation to set the correct tax or subsidy","Firms and consumers respond to price signals in predictable ways","Utility can be compared across people, at least approximately"],"influence":"Pigouvian taxes inform policy on carbon emissions, fuel, tobacco, sugary drinks and congestion. In 1960 Ronald Coase challenged Pigou’s approach in The Problem of Social Cost, arguing that with clearly defined property rights and low bargaining costs the affected parties might reach an efficient outcome themselves. The resulting debate, including the use of tradable permits as an alternative, continues to shape environmental economics. Keynes used Pigou’s work as an example of the classical approach he was challenging.","criticisms":["Ronald Coase argued that externalities are reciprocal and that, where transaction costs are low, assigning property rights may resolve them without taxation; Pigou’s framework, he said, overlooked this.","Setting the correct tax requires knowing the size of the external cost, which is often uncertain or disputed.","Public choice economists argue that governments may set taxes for revenue or political reasons rather than to correct the externality, so intervention can itself fail.","Interpersonal comparisons of utility, which support his case for redistribution, are rejected by economists who regard them as unscientific."],"modern":"Pigou’s ideas sit behind debates about carbon pricing, congestion charging and taxes on demerit goods. Economists often compare Pigouvian taxes with cap-and-trade schemes, regulation and nudges, weighing information requirements, political feasibility and distributional effects. When a student draws a negative externality diagram and shows how a tax could move output to the socially optimum level, they are applying his analysis, and evaluation usually turns on the practical problems he and his critics identified.","works":[{"t":"The Economics of Welfare","y":1920}],"honours":null,"misread":"Students sometimes think a Pigouvian tax aims to eliminate the harmful activity. The aim is to reduce output to the socially optimum level, where marginal social cost equals marginal social benefit, which is usually above zero.","subs":["2.8","2.7","3.4"],"kc":["Intervention","Efficiency","Economic well-being"],"terms":["Pigouvian tax","Externality","Marginal social cost","Marginal social benefit","Socially optimum output","Property rights"],"dg":["negprod","tax"],"question":"Is a carbon tax or a system of tradable permits the better way to apply Pigou’s insight in an economy you have studied, and why?","connects":[{"id":"marshall","why":"Pigou built his welfare economics on Marshall’s consumer surplus and external effects."},{"id":"ostrom","why":"Ostrom showed that some shared-resource problems can be managed without either taxes or privatisation."},{"id":"keynes","why":"Keynes cast Pigou as a representative of the classical view of unemployment."}]},{"id":"keynes","name":"John Maynard Keynes","life":"1883–1946","era":"Keynesian macroeconomics","headline":"Keynes argued that an economy can remain stuck with high unemployment because total spending is too low, and that government can act to raise aggregate demand.","context":"Keynes was a Cambridge economist, Treasury official and public intellectual. After the First World War he criticised the reparations imposed on Germany as economically damaging. In the 1920s Britain suffered persistent unemployment, and from 1929 the Great Depression brought mass unemployment across industrial economies. The prevailing view held that flexible wages and prices would restore full employment, and that governments should balance their budgets. Keynes doubted that wage cuts would work, and asked why unemployment could persist for years. His answer required a theory of output as a whole, which helped create macroeconomics as a separate field.","ideas":[{"h":"Effective demand","p":"Keynes argued that output and employment are determined by aggregate demand: planned spending by households, firms, government and foreign buyers. If that spending falls, firms cut output and jobs. An economy can settle at an equilibrium below full employment, a deflationary gap, with no strong force pushing it back. Cutting wages may reduce workers’ incomes and spending, so it may not cure unemployment."},{"h":"The multiplier","p":"Richard Kahn developed the employment multiplier in 1931, and Keynes made it part of his theory. An initial rise in spending becomes income for others, who spend part of it, generating further rounds of income. The total rise in national income is a multiple of the initial injection, depending on the share of extra income that leaks into saving, taxes and imports. The Keynesian multiplier is HL only in the IB course."},{"h":"Uncertainty, expectations and investment","p":"Investment depends on firms’ expectations about uncertain future profits, which Keynes argued cannot be reduced to calculable probabilities. Confidence, which he described as ‘animal spirits’, can shift sharply, making investment volatile. A collapse in business confidence can therefore start a downturn even without any change in underlying productive capacity. Falling investment then lowers incomes through the multiplier."},{"h":"Money, interest and the paradox of thrift","p":"Keynes argued that interest rates reflect the demand to hold money as a liquid asset, not simply saving and investment. In a deep slump, lower rates may do little to stimulate borrowing. If every household tries to save more at once, total spending and incomes fall, so total saving may not rise. Government spending financed by borrowing can then fill the gap in demand."}],"contribution":"Keynes made aggregate demand the starting point for explaining fluctuations in output and employment, and gave governments a framework for stabilisation through fiscal and monetary policy. The AD–AS model, the circular flow with injections and withdrawals, the multiplier and the idea of an output gap all draw on his work. He also showed that what is sensible for one household, such as saving more, can have different effects when everyone does it.","assumptions":["Wages and prices are slow to adjust downward, especially in the short run","Investment depends heavily on uncertain expectations","Spare capacity exists, so higher demand raises output more than prices","Consumption depends mainly on current income"],"influence":"After the Second World War many governments adopted demand management, aiming to stabilise output and employment through fiscal policy. Keynes helped design the Bretton Woods system of international monetary cooperation in 1944. In the 1970s, rising inflation together with high unemployment led many economists, including Friedman, to challenge Keynesian policy. Large fiscal and monetary responses to the 2008 financial crisis and the 2020 pandemic drew heavily on Keynesian reasoning.","criticisms":["Friedman and others argued that discretionary policy, monetary as well as fiscal, acts with long and variable lags and may destabilise rather than stabilise output.","The experience of rising inflation alongside high unemployment in the 1970s was hard to explain with early Keynesian models that assumed a stable trade-off between inflation and unemployment (the Phillips curve, HL only in the IB course).","Government borrowing to raise spending may crowd out private investment by pushing up interest rates, reducing the net effect on demand (crowding out is HL only in the IB course, 3.6).","Economists in the new classical tradition argued that his models lacked a consistent account of how households and firms form expectations."],"modern":"Keynesian reasoning frames much of the discussion of recessions, stimulus packages and austerity. Debates continue about the size of fiscal multipliers, whether low interest rates limit monetary policy, and how large public debt can safely become. New Keynesian models combine his emphasis on sticky prices and demand with forward-looking expectations, and they shape how many central banks think about policy.","works":[{"t":"The Economic Consequences of the Peace","y":1919},{"t":"The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money","y":1936}],"honours":null,"misread":"Students often say Keynes favoured permanent budget deficits. His case for deficit spending applied to periods of weak demand and spare capacity; in a boom, Keynesian reasoning points towards restraint.","subs":["3.2","3.6","3.1"],"kc":["Intervention","Economic well-being","Change"],"terms":["Aggregate demand","Keynesian multiplier","Deflationary gap","Keynesian AS curve","Fiscal policy","Crowding out"],"dg":["defgap","multiplier"],"question":"How far does the size of the multiplier in an economy you have studied depend on leakages such as imports and taxes, and what does that imply for fiscal stimulus?","connects":[{"id":"friedman","why":"Friedman’s monetarism was the leading challenge to Keynesian demand management."},{"id":"hayek","why":"Hayek debated Keynes in the 1930s over the causes of slumps and the role of policy."},{"id":"robinson","why":"Robinson was among the Cambridge economists who helped develop and extend his theory."}]},{"id":"robinson","name":"Joan Robinson","life":"1903–1983","era":"Imperfect competition and post-Keynesian economics","headline":"Robinson showed how firms with market power set prices and wages, introducing the term monopsony, and later challenged core assumptions of mainstream growth and capital theory.","context":"Robinson studied and taught at Cambridge, in a period when the subject was dominated by Marshall’s followers. In the late 1920s Piero Sraffa pointed out that Marshall’s theory of perfectly competitive firms struggled to account for industries with falling costs and branded products. Many real firms clearly influenced their own prices. During the Depression, Robinson was part of a group of young Cambridge economists who discussed Keynes’s ideas while he developed his theory of employment. Throughout her career she questioned whether prevailing theory described how capitalist economies actually work.","ideas":[{"h":"Imperfect competition","p":"Robinson analysed firms facing downward-sloping demand curves, so that selling more requires cutting price. Such a firm maximises profit where marginal revenue equals marginal cost, and charges a price above marginal cost. Output is lower and price higher than under perfect competition. Edward Chamberlin developed a related theory of monopolistic competition in the same year, independently."},{"h":"Monopsony","p":"Robinson introduced the term monopsony for a market with a single buyer, such as a dominant employer in a town. Because hiring more workers requires raising the wage for everyone, the marginal cost of labour exceeds the wage. The firm hires fewer workers and pays less than in a competitive labour market. In this setting a well-set minimum wage could raise both wages and employment."},{"h":"Price discrimination","p":"Robinson worked out when a firm with market power can charge different prices to separate groups of buyers, and how it would set them. Prices tend to be higher for groups with less elastic demand. Whether discrimination reduces or increases welfare depends on its effect on total output, which she showed could go either way."},{"h":"Critique of capital and growth theory","p":"Later, Robinson led the Cambridge side of the ‘Cambridge capital controversy’, arguing that capital cannot be measured as a single quantity independent of prices and the rate of profit. If so, simple models in which output depends on a stock of ‘capital’ face logical problems. She also stressed historical time and uncertainty, influencing post-Keynesian economics."}],"contribution":"Robinson’s work helped move economics beyond the polar cases of perfect competition and pure monopoly, and made marginal revenue a standard tool. Monopsony is now central to labour economics and to debates about minimum wages. Her analysis of price discrimination remains standard. Her later critiques sharpened debate about the foundations of growth theory, even though many economists continued to use the models she criticised.","assumptions":["Firms aim to maximise profit by equating marginal revenue and marginal cost","Firms face downward-sloping demand curves rather than a horizontal one","Buyers can be separated into groups with different elasticities when price discrimination is possible","In monopsony, a single buyer faces an upward-sloping supply of labour"],"influence":"Robinson’s models became standard in microeconomics courses. Monopsony analysis has gained renewed attention in research on employer concentration and in the minimum wage debate, where it offers one explanation for why some studies find small employment effects. Competition authorities consider buyer power as well as seller power. Her post-Keynesian work influenced economists who emphasise demand, uncertainty and distribution in growth.","criticisms":["Her static models of imperfect competition say little about strategic interaction between rival firms, which game theory later addressed in oligopoly.","In the capital controversy, many economists accepted her logical point but continued to use aggregate production functions as useful approximations.","Her sympathetic assessments of some planned economies were later widely judged to have been too favourable."],"modern":"Robinson’s ideas are directly relevant to HL subtopic 2.11 on market power. Debates about large digital platforms, supermarkets’ power over suppliers and employers’ wage-setting power in some labour markets all use her concepts. Monopsony provides a counterweight to the simple competitive prediction that a minimum wage reduces employment, which matters when evaluating labour market policies.","works":[{"t":"The Economics of Imperfect Competition","y":1933}],"honours":null,"misread":"Students sometimes treat monopsony and monopoly as the same. A monopolist is a single seller with power over price; a monopsonist is a single buyer, often an employer, with power over the price it pays.","subs":["2.11","3.3","3.4"],"kc":["Efficiency","Equity","Intervention"],"terms":["Market power","Monopoly","Marginal revenue","Minimum wage","Barriers to entry","Price maker"],"dg":["monopoly","labour"],"question":"Could monopsony help to explain why a minimum wage in a labour market you have studied had a smaller effect on employment than a competitive model predicts?","connects":[{"id":"marshall","why":"Robinson rebuilt Marshall’s theory of the firm for markets with price-setting firms."},{"id":"keynes","why":"She was part of the Cambridge group that helped develop Keynes’s theory."},{"id":"marx","why":"She wrote a study of Marx’s economics that engaged critically with his ideas."}]},{"id":"hayek","name":"Friedrich August von Hayek","life":"1899–1992","era":"Austrian school","headline":"Hayek argued that prices transmit dispersed knowledge that no central planner could gather, making the problem of economic coordination as much about information as about incentives.","context":"Hayek was born in Vienna and trained in the Austrian tradition associated with Carl Menger and Ludwig von Mises. He moved to the London School of Economics in 1931. In the interwar years, many economists believed that central planning could match or outperform markets, and some argued that a socialist economy could replicate market prices through calculation. Hayek, following Mises, joined this ‘socialist calculation debate’. He also argued with Keynes about the causes of the Depression. Writing during the Second World War, he worried that wartime economic controls would become a permanent feature of peacetime economies.","ideas":[{"h":"Prices as information","p":"Knowledge about local conditions, such as a sudden shortage of a material or a spare machine in one factory, is scattered among millions of people and often cannot be written down. A change in price summarises that knowledge and signals it to everyone who uses the good, prompting them to economise or supply more without knowing why. The price mechanism is, in this view, a system for communicating information."},{"h":"The knowledge problem in planning","p":"A central planner would need to collect and process this dispersed, changing knowledge to allocate resources well. Hayek argued that this is not just a matter of computing power: much relevant knowledge is tacit and specific to time and place. Planning therefore tends to produce shortages, surpluses and poor quality, because the planner cannot see what market prices would reveal."},{"h":"Spontaneous order","p":"Many useful social institutions, such as language, money, common law and markets, emerged from human action without being designed by anyone. Hayek argued that such orders can coordinate far more activity than deliberate planning. Attempts to redesign them wholesale may destroy knowledge embedded in rules that have evolved over time, even when those rules seem arbitrary."},{"h":"Credit, malinvestment and the cycle","p":"In his business-cycle theory, Hayek argued that when banks or central banks push interest rates below the level consistent with saving, firms invest in long-term projects that are not sustainable. The boom rests on distorted price signals, and the bust is the painful correction. This put him at odds with Keynes, who emphasised insufficient demand."}],"contribution":"Hayek’s main lasting contribution is the idea that the economic problem is partly one of using knowledge that no single person holds. This reframed the comparison between markets and planning around information, not just efficiency in a static model. His work fed into later research on information, institutions and incentives. Within the IB course, his argument offers a theoretical explanation of why the price mechanism can allocate resources without central direction.","assumptions":["Relevant economic knowledge is dispersed, local and often tacit","Prices in competitive markets reflect changing conditions of scarcity","Individuals respond to price signals in pursuing their own plans","Rules and institutions that evolved over time often embody useful knowledge"],"influence":"The Road to Serfdom reached a large audience and influenced debates about the role of the state after 1945. Hayek helped found the Mont Pelerin Society in 1947, a network of liberal economists and thinkers. His ideas were cited by policymakers in the 1970s and 1980s who favoured deregulation and privatisation. Economists who study information, from those who analyse markets to those working on mechanism design, engage with his knowledge argument.","criticisms":["Critics argue that prices convey useful information only if markets are reasonably complete and competitive; with externalities, asymmetric information or market power, they may send misleading signals.","The Road to Serfdom’s warning that extensive economic planning leads towards authoritarian rule is disputed, since several countries built large welfare states while remaining democracies.","Keynesian economists argued that his business-cycle theory offered little practical guidance during a deep depression, when allowing the slump to run its course carried heavy costs."],"modern":"Hayek’s knowledge problem is relevant to debates about whether data and algorithms could allow more central coordination of economic activity, and to the design of price-based policies such as auctions and tradable permits. His concern with credit-fuelled booms is cited in discussions of asset bubbles after periods of low interest rates. Students can use his arguments to evaluate planned economies and government price controls, while weighing them against the market failures that limit price signals.","works":[{"t":"The Road to Serfdom","y":1944},{"t":"The Use of Knowledge in Society","y":1945}],"honours":"Nobel memorial prize in economic sciences, 1974, shared with Gunnar Myrdal","misread":"Students sometimes describe Hayek as opposing all government. He accepted a role for the state in providing a legal framework, some public goods and a minimum safety net; his objection was to central direction of economic activity.","subs":["1.1","2.3","2.7"],"kc":["Choice","Efficiency","Intervention"],"terms":["Price mechanism","Planned economy","Free market economy","Price ceiling","Deregulation"],"dg":["ds","ceiling"],"question":"Using Hayek’s idea of prices as signals, how might a binding price ceiling on a staple good affect the information available to producers and consumers?","connects":[{"id":"keynes","why":"Hayek and Keynes debated the causes of the Depression and the role of policy."},{"id":"smith","why":"Hayek extended Smith’s idea of an unplanned market order."},{"id":"friedman","why":"Both were leading twentieth-century advocates of market economies, though they differed on monetary theory."}]},{"id":"friedman","name":"Milton Friedman","life":"1912–2006","era":"Monetarism and the Chicago school","headline":"Friedman put the money supply and expectations at the centre of macroeconomics, arguing that there is no lasting trade-off between inflation and unemployment.","context":"Friedman spent most of his career at the University of Chicago. In the decades after 1945, Keynesian ideas dominated macroeconomic policy, and many economists believed governments could choose a point on a stable trade-off between inflation and unemployment. Friedman challenged this. He studied the history of money in detail and argued that the Great Depression had been worsened by monetary contraction. By the late 1960s he warned that attempts to hold unemployment below its natural level would bring rising inflation, a prediction that seemed borne out by the experience of the 1970s.","ideas":[{"h":"Money and inflation","p":"Building on the quantity theory of money, Friedman argued that sustained inflation results from the money supply growing faster than real output. If the central bank expands money too quickly, spending rises, and once the economy is at capacity this shows up mainly in prices. He recommended that monetary authorities aim for steady, predictable growth of the money supply rather than discretionary adjustment."},{"h":"The natural rate and expectations","p":"In his 1968 presidential address to the American Economic Association, Friedman argued that unemployment tends towards a natural rate determined by real factors in the labour market. Expansionary policy can reduce unemployment below this rate only temporarily, while workers’ expectations of inflation lag behind. Once expectations adjust, unemployment returns to the natural rate at a higher inflation rate. Edmund Phelps reached similar conclusions independently."},{"h":"The Great Contraction","p":"With Anna Schwartz, Friedman argued in A Monetary History of the United States that the Federal Reserve allowed the money supply to fall sharply in the early 1930s as banks failed. In their account, this turned a recession into a depression. The interpretation shifted blame from a failure of markets to a failure of monetary policy, and influenced how central banks respond to financial crises."},{"h":"Permanent income","p":"Friedman argued that households base consumption on their expected long-run income, not just current income. A temporary tax cut or one-off payment may therefore have a smaller effect on spending than a permanent change. This challenged the simple Keynesian consumption function and implied that short-term fiscal stimulus could be less powerful than assumed."}],"contribution":"Friedman made expectations and monetary policy central to macroeconomics. The expectations-augmented Phillips curve, distinguishing the short run from the long run, is now standard; in the IB course the Phillips curve is HL only. His historical work changed views of the Depression, and his permanent income hypothesis reshaped the study of consumption. He also argued for flexible exchange rates and many market-based policies.","assumptions":["The demand for money is relatively stable, so changes in the money supply have predictable effects on spending","Expectations of inflation adapt to experience","The economy tends towards a natural rate of unemployment in the long run","Long and variable lags make discretionary policy unreliable"],"influence":"Several central banks in the late 1970s and early 1980s adopted targets for money supply growth, influenced by monetarist ideas. The long-run vertical Phillips curve became part of mainstream macroeconomics, and the emphasis on anchoring inflation expectations underlies modern inflation targeting. Friedman also argued publicly for floating exchange rates, school vouchers and a negative income tax, and his writing for general readers shaped political debates about the scope of government.","criticisms":["The relationship between money and spending became unstable in the 1980s as financial markets changed, and most central banks abandoned money supply targets.","Critics argue that the natural rate is hard to estimate and may itself shift with past unemployment, so the long-run Phillips curve may be less vertical than he claimed.","Rational expectations economists argued that adaptive expectations allowed people to be fooled repeatedly, a weakness in his framework.","Keynesian economists argued that a stable money rule would leave policymakers unable to respond to large shocks to demand."],"modern":"Most central banks now target inflation directly rather than money supply growth, but Friedman’s emphasis on expectations shapes how they communicate. His reading of the Depression influenced the large monetary responses to the 2008 crisis. Debates about whether loose monetary policy contributes to inflation, and about the long-run Phillips curve, continue to draw on his work, as do discussions of income support schemes resembling his negative income tax.","works":[{"t":"A Monetary History of the United States, 1867–1960 (with Anna Schwartz)","y":1963}],"honours":"Nobel memorial prize in economic sciences, 1976","misread":"Students sometimes say Friedman argued that unemployment cannot be reduced. He argued that demand-side policy cannot keep unemployment below the natural rate in the long run; supply-side changes to the labour market may lower the natural rate itself.","subs":["3.3","3.5","3.7"],"kc":["Change","Intervention","Choice"],"terms":["Phillips curve","Natural rate of unemployment","Monetary policy","Inflation targeting","Inflation","Central bank"],"dg":["phillips"],"question":"To what extent does recent experience in an economy you have studied support the idea that there is no long-run trade-off between inflation and unemployment?","connects":[{"id":"keynes","why":"Friedman’s work was the leading challenge to postwar Keynesian policy."},{"id":"hayek","why":"Both defended market economies, though Friedman’s monetary theory differed from Hayek’s."},{"id":"stiglitz","why":"Stiglitz’s work on information failures challenged the confidence in markets associated with Chicago economics."}]},{"id":"sen","name":"Amartya Sen","life":"born 1933","era":"Welfare economics and development","headline":"Sen redefined development and poverty in terms of what people are able to do and be, and showed that famines can occur without a fall in food supply.","context":"Sen grew up in Bengal and witnessed the Bengal famine of 1943 as a child. After independence, India and many newly independent countries treated development largely as a matter of raising national income. Welfare economics, meanwhile, judged outcomes mainly by income or utility, and social choice theory had produced discouraging results about combining individual preferences into collective decisions. Sen worked on all of these. He asked why people starve in societies with food available, what development should aim at, and how to make sound judgements about inequality and well-being when people differ in their needs and circumstances.","ideas":[{"h":"Entitlements and famine","p":"Sen argued that famines result from a collapse in people’s entitlements: their ability to command food through what they own, produce, earn or receive. In the 1943 Bengal famine, he argued, food supply had not fallen enough to explain mass starvation; instead, rising food prices and lost earnings left rural labourers unable to buy food. Starvation is about people not having enough food, not necessarily about there not being enough food."},{"h":"The capability approach","p":"Income is a means, not an end. Sen argued that well-being should be judged by people’s capabilities: their real freedom to achieve valued functionings such as being healthy, educated and able to take part in community life. Two people with the same income may have very different capabilities, for example if one has a disability or lives where public services are poor."},{"h":"Development as freedom","p":"Sen defined development as the expansion of substantive freedoms, including political liberties, economic opportunities, social provision, transparency and protective security. These freedoms reinforce one another. He argued that democracy and a free press help prevent famines, because governments facing elections and public criticism have strong incentives to act. Freedom is thus both the goal of development and a means of achieving it."},{"h":"Inequality and missing women","p":"Sen developed measures of inequality and poverty sensitive to how income is distributed among the poor. He also drew attention to ‘missing women’: in some regions the ratio of women to men is much lower than would be expected given equal care, reflecting unequal treatment in nutrition, health care and, in some cases, sex-selective practices."}],"contribution":"Sen widened the economic evaluation of well-being beyond income. The capability approach provided the philosophical basis for the Human Development Index, introduced in 1990, which Sen developed with Mahbub ul Haq. His entitlement analysis changed how economists and agencies study famine and food security. His work in social choice and on poverty measurement shaped how inequality and deprivation are assessed, including in multidimensional measures.","assumptions":["Well-being depends on capabilities, not only on income or utility","People differ in their ability to convert income into well-being","Access to food depends on entitlements, which can fail even when food is available","Political freedoms affect economic outcomes through accountability"],"influence":"The United Nations Development Programme’s Human Development Reports drew directly on the capability approach. Agencies now treat famine prevention partly as a matter of protecting incomes, for example through public works or cash transfers, rather than only supplying food. Composite indicators, such as the Multidimensional Poverty Index, reflect his argument that deprivation has several dimensions. Philosophers, notably Martha Nussbaum, have developed the capability approach further.","criticisms":["Critics argue that the capability approach does not specify which capabilities matter most, making it hard to apply consistently without value judgements.","Some historians argue that the entitlement approach underplays the role of food supply shortfalls, war and government policy in particular famines, including Bengal in 1943.","Composite indices inspired by his work, such as the HDI, involve arbitrary choices about indicators and weights, which Sen himself acknowledged."],"modern":"Sen’s work informs how students evaluate development, poverty and the limits of GDP per capita. Debates about whether to use income, consumption or multidimensional measures, about the role of democracy in development, and about how to prevent hunger during price shocks all draw on his ideas. In IB terms, his capability approach underlies the Human Development Index and the discussion of composite indicators in 4.8.","works":[{"t":"Poverty and Famines: An Essay on Entitlement and Deprivation","y":1981},{"t":"Development as Freedom","y":1999}],"honours":"Nobel memorial prize in economic sciences, 1998","misread":"Students sometimes think Sen dismissed income as unimportant. He treats income as an important means to well-being, but argues that it is an incomplete measure because people convert income into capabilities at different rates.","subs":["4.8","3.4","4.7"],"kc":["Economic well-being","Equity","Sustainability"],"terms":["Human Development Index","Economic development","Composite indicator","Multidimensional Poverty Index","Absolute poverty","Gender Inequality Index"],"dg":[],"question":"How well does the Human Development Index capture Sen’s idea of capabilities, and what dimensions of development does it leave out for a country you have studied?","connects":[{"id":"malthus","why":"Sen’s entitlement analysis challenged explanations of famine based only on food supply."},{"id":"banerjee","why":"Banerjee and Duflo share his focus on the lived conditions of poor households."},{"id":"smith","why":"Sen has written extensively on Smith’s moral philosophy and his concern for the poor."}]},{"id":"ostrom","name":"Elinor Ostrom","life":"1933–2012","era":"Institutional economics and the commons","headline":"Ostrom showed through field evidence that communities can manage shared resources sustainably through their own rules, without either privatisation or central control.","context":"Ostrom trained as a political scientist and worked at Indiana University, where she founded a research workshop with Vincent Ostrom. In 1968 Garrett Hardin popularised the ‘tragedy of the commons’: when a resource such as a pasture or fishery is open to all, each user has an incentive to overuse it, and the resource is depleted. The standard policy responses were to privatise the resource or to have government regulate it. Ostrom observed that many real communities had managed fisheries, forests, grazing land and irrigation systems for long periods without either. She set out to explain when and why such arrangements work.","ideas":[{"h":"Beyond the tragedy of the commons","p":"Common pool resources are rivalrous but hard to exclude people from, which creates a risk of overuse. Ostrom accepted this risk but rejected the conclusion that only markets or the state can prevent depletion. Users who can communicate, build trust and agree rules can sometimes avoid the tragedy. Whether they do depends on the institutions they create."},{"h":"Design principles","p":"From many case studies, including Swiss alpine meadows, Japanese forests and irrigation systems in Spain and the Philippines, Ostrom identified features shared by durable arrangements. These included clear boundaries, rules suited to local conditions, participation of users in making rules, monitoring by people accountable to users, graduated sanctions, low-cost ways to resolve conflicts and recognition by outside authorities."},{"h":"Collective self-governance","p":"Ostrom showed that users can monitor and enforce rules themselves, often more cheaply and effectively than outside regulators who lack local knowledge. Self-governance relies on reciprocity and reputation: people cooperate if they expect others to do so, and sanctions start small. This links economic outcomes to trust and social norms, not only to prices or laws."},{"h":"Polycentric governance","p":"Rather than a single authority, Ostrom favoured systems with several overlapping centres of decision-making at local, regional and national levels. Problems at different scales can be handled at the level best suited to them. She applied this thinking to large problems such as climate change, arguing that action at many levels could complement global agreements."}],"contribution":"Ostrom broadened the range of solutions economists consider for market failure in common pool resources. She showed that institutions created by resource users can be an alternative to privatisation and state regulation, and she gave a systematic, evidence-based account of when such institutions succeed. Her combination of field studies, laboratory experiments and game theory also influenced the methods used to study cooperation.","assumptions":["Resource users can communicate and repeatedly interact with each other","Users have local knowledge about the resource that outsiders lack","Trust and reciprocity can sustain cooperation when rules are seen as fair","Groups and resources are of a manageable size with identifiable boundaries"],"influence":"Ostrom’s work changed how development agencies and governments approach community forestry, fisheries and water management, supporting forms of co-management between communities and the state. Environmental economists now consider community institutions alongside taxes, permits and property rights. Her ideas also influenced research on the governance of knowledge and digital commons. She was the first woman to receive the economics prize.","criticisms":["Her design principles were drawn mainly from small, local resources; how far they apply to global commons such as the atmosphere or oceans, with millions of users, is debated.","Critics note that community management can entrench local elites or exclude outsiders, raising equity concerns.","Some economists argue that her case-study method makes it hard to separate the effect of institutions from other local conditions."],"modern":"Ostrom’s work is directly relevant to subtopic 2.8 on common pool resources and to 4.7 on sustainable development. Debates about overfishing, groundwater depletion, community forestry and climate cooperation draw on her findings. When evaluating responses to a common pool problem, students can compare government regulation and tradable permits with collective self-governance, and consider the conditions that make each more likely to work.","works":[{"t":"Governing the Commons: The Evolution of Institutions for Collective Action","y":1990}],"honours":"Nobel memorial prize in economic sciences, 2009, shared with Oliver Williamson; the first woman to receive the economics prize","misread":"Students sometimes conclude from Ostrom that government has no role in managing commons. She argued that community rules work best under certain conditions, and that recognition and support from higher levels of government are often part of successful arrangements.","subs":["2.8","4.7"],"kc":["Sustainability","Interdependence","Intervention"],"terms":["Common pool resource","Tragedy of the commons","Collective self-governance","Property rights","Sustainable development"],"dg":["cpr"],"question":"Which of Ostrom’s design principles are present, or missing, in the management of a common pool resource you have studied?","connects":[{"id":"pigou","why":"Ostrom’s approach offers an alternative to Pigouvian taxes for some shared-resource problems."},{"id":"hayek","why":"Both stressed local knowledge and institutions that evolve without central design."},{"id":"acemoglu","why":"Both treat institutions as the key to explaining economic outcomes."}]},{"id":"stiglitz","name":"Joseph E. Stiglitz","life":"born 1943","era":"Information economics","headline":"Stiglitz showed that when buyers and sellers hold different information, markets can fail in systematic ways, so that prices alone may not produce efficient outcomes.","context":"Stiglitz studied at Amherst College and the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, and has taught at several leading universities, including Columbia. Standard general-equilibrium theory, developed in the mid-twentieth century, showed that competitive markets are efficient under strict conditions, including that everyone has the information they need. From the late 1960s, a group of economists including George Akerlof and Michael Spence began asking what happens when that condition fails. Stiglitz explored how information problems affect insurance, credit and labour markets. He later served as chair of the US Council of Economic Advisers and as chief economist of the World Bank.","ideas":[{"h":"Screening","p":"When one party knows less than the other, it can design choices that lead the better-informed side to reveal information. Stiglitz, with Michael Rothschild, showed that insurers can offer contracts with different deductibles and premiums: low-risk customers choose high deductibles and low premiums, high-risk customers the reverse. Screening can reduce adverse selection, but the resulting market may still be inefficient, and may sometimes fail to reach an equilibrium."},{"h":"Credit rationing","p":"With Andrew Weiss, Stiglitz argued that banks may not raise interest rates to clear excess demand for loans. A higher rate can drive away safer borrowers and encourage riskier projects, reducing the bank’s expected return. Banks therefore ration credit at a given rate, so some creditworthy borrowers cannot borrow at all. This helps explain why small firms and poor households may lack access to finance."},{"h":"Efficiency wages","p":"With Carl Shapiro, Stiglitz showed that if employers cannot perfectly monitor effort, they may pay above the market-clearing wage to make job loss costly and discourage shirking. Because many firms do this, some unemployment persists even in equilibrium. The model offers one explanation of involuntary unemployment in which wages stay above the market-clearing level because of information problems, not because of minimum wages or union bargaining."},{"h":"The limits of the invisible hand","p":"Stiglitz and Bruce Greenwald argued that when information is imperfect or markets are incomplete, market outcomes are generally not efficient, even with competition. This weakens the general presumption that competitive markets are efficient and suggests a potential role for well-designed intervention, though it does not show that any particular intervention will help."}],"contribution":"Stiglitz helped build the economics of information, showing how asymmetric information shapes contracts, prices and the structure of markets. Concepts such as screening, credit rationing and efficiency wages are now standard in microeconomics, finance and labour economics. In the IB course, the analysis of asymmetric information in 2.10, which is HL only, draws on the work he shared with Akerlof and Spence.","assumptions":["One party to a transaction has relevant information the other lacks","Actions such as effort or risk-taking cannot be perfectly observed","Prices and contract terms affect the mix of people who trade, not only how much is traded","Markets for some risks are missing or incomplete"],"influence":"Information economics changed the design of insurance, lending and employment contracts and of regulation in those markets. Stiglitz’s arguments about credit markets informed debates on financial regulation and microfinance. As World Bank chief economist and later as a writer, he criticised policies associated with the IMF in the 1990s, arguing that rapid capital-market liberalisation and austerity worsened crises in several countries. He has also written widely on inequality.","criticisms":["Critics argue that showing markets with imperfect information are generally inefficient does not establish that government, which also has limited information, can do better.","His criticisms of the IMF’s handling of the Asian financial crisis were contested by IMF economists, including Kenneth Rogoff, who defended the policies.","Efficiency wage models are hard to test directly, and the evidence on how much they contribute to unemployment is mixed."],"modern":"Questions about insurance markets, used-car platforms, online reviews, credit scoring and data privacy are applications of information economics. Regulation that requires disclosure, licensing or minimum standards responds to the problems Stiglitz analysed. Students evaluating government responses to asymmetric information can use his work to explain why markets may fail, and also to ask whether regulators have better information than the market participants they oversee.","works":[{"t":"Globalization and Its Discontents","y":2002}],"honours":"Nobel memorial prize in economic sciences, 2001, shared with George Akerlof and Michael Spence","misread":"Students sometimes treat asymmetric information as simply ‘one side being dishonest’. The problem arises even with honest parties, because hidden characteristics or hidden actions change who participates and how they behave, which can shrink or close a market.","subs":["2.10","3.3","4.10"],"kc":["Efficiency","Intervention","Economic well-being"],"terms":["Asymmetric information","Adverse selection","Moral hazard","Screening","Signalling","Microfinance"],"dg":["asym"],"question":"How effectively does regulation in a market you have studied, such as health insurance or used cars, deal with the information problems Stiglitz identified?","connects":[{"id":"hayek","why":"Both made information central, but drew opposite conclusions about markets."},{"id":"friedman","why":"Stiglitz’s work challenged the view that competitive markets are generally efficient."},{"id":"banerjee","why":"Credit rationing helps explain the finance constraints poor households face, which Banerjee studies."}]},{"id":"banerjee","name":"Abhijit Banerjee","life":"born 1961","era":"Experimental development economics","headline":"Banerjee helped turn development economics towards testing specific policies with randomised experiments and towards understanding the economic lives of poor households in detail.","context":"Banerjee was born in India and studied in India and at Harvard, before joining the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. By the 1990s, development economics was divided between broad claims that aid either worked or failed, and cross-country comparisons that struggled to separate cause from correlation. Banerjee, whose early theoretical work examined how information and incentives shape credit and occupational choice in poor economies, became frustrated with the lack of reliable evidence on what specific programmes achieve. With Esther Duflo and Sendhil Mullainathan he founded the Abdul Latif Jameel Poverty Action Lab (J-PAL) in 2003 to promote rigorous evaluation of anti-poverty policies.","ideas":[{"h":"Randomised controlled trials","p":"An RCT randomly assigns a programme to some households, schools or villages and not to others, then compares outcomes. Because assignment is random, differences between the groups can be attributed to the programme rather than to pre-existing differences. Banerjee argued that this method can answer narrow, practical questions, such as whether remedial tutoring raises learning, more convincingly than broad comparisons across countries."},{"h":"The economic lives of the poor","p":"Banerjee and Duflo documented how poor households in many countries spend, borrow, save and take risks. They found choices that seem puzzling, such as spending on festivals rather than food, often make sense given constraints, risks and the absence of insurance. Poor households face the same human tendencies as everyone else, but with less room for error and fewer institutions to fall back on."},{"h":"Poverty traps, case by case","p":"Rather than asking whether a single poverty trap explains global poverty, Banerjee argued that traps may exist in some domains and not in others. Small investments in nutrition, health or schooling may lead to large gains, or not, depending on the setting. Whether a trap exists is an empirical question to be tested."},{"h":"Political economy of service delivery","p":"Banerjee examined why public services often fail the poor even when funded, pointing to absenteeism, weak accountability and capture by local elites. Experiments testing ways to improve delivery, such as information campaigns or changes in monitoring, showed how institutional details within a single programme affect outcomes. Funding alone, on this view, tells us little about what reaches the poor."}],"contribution":"Banerjee helped establish experimental methods as a mainstream tool in development economics and showed how close study of poor households can challenge assumptions on all sides of the aid debate. His work turned attention from grand theories of development towards the design and implementation of specific programmes. It also connected development economics with behavioural economics, showing how constraints and human limitations interact.","assumptions":["Random assignment creates comparable treatment and control groups","Effects measured in one setting can inform policy elsewhere if conditions are similar","Poor households respond rationally to their constraints, within normal behavioural limits","Many development questions can be broken into specific, testable policy choices"],"influence":"J-PAL’s network of researchers has evaluated many programmes in health, education, finance and governance, and some results have informed government policy, such as the expansion of remedial teaching programmes in parts of India. Development agencies and charities increasingly require rigorous evaluation. Banerjee’s work also shaped debates on microfinance, where experimental evidence suggested more modest effects than early advocates claimed.","criticisms":["Angus Deaton and others argued that RCTs are not uniquely reliable and that results from one context may not generalise to others, a problem of external validity.","Critics argue that the method suits small, testable interventions and may divert attention from larger questions about institutions, macroeconomic policy and structural change.","Experiments on poor households raise ethical questions about consent and about withholding a potentially beneficial programme from a control group."],"modern":"Banerjee’s approach shapes how governments, charities and aid agencies design and evaluate programmes such as cash transfers, school interventions and health campaigns. In the IB course, it connects 4.10 on development strategies with 1.2, where the guide asks how economists approach the world, including the use of evidence. Students can use his work to evaluate claims about microfinance, aid and poverty traps, and to ask what kind of evidence would test a policy claim.","works":[{"t":"Poor Economics (with Esther Duflo)","y":2011}],"honours":"Nobel memorial prize in economic sciences, 2019, shared with Esther Duflo and Michael Kremer","misread":"Students sometimes think Banerjee claims that poor people make irrational choices. His work argues that poor households often make sensible choices given tight constraints and risks, and that the same behavioural biases found among richer people matter more when there is little margin for error.","subs":["4.10","1.2","3.4"],"kc":["Economic well-being","Equity","Intervention"],"terms":["Microfinance","Poverty trap","Foreign aid","Behavioural economics","Absolute poverty"],"dg":[],"question":"What could, and could not, a randomised controlled trial tell us about the effectiveness of a development strategy you have studied?","connects":[{"id":"duflo","why":"His closest collaborator, with whom he founded J-PAL and wrote Poor Economics."},{"id":"sen","why":"Both focus on the conditions and choices of poor households."},{"id":"acemoglu","why":"Acemoglu’s emphasis on institutions contrasts with their focus on specific programmes."}]},{"id":"duflo","name":"Esther Duflo","life":"born 1972","era":"Experimental development economics","headline":"Duflo showed how carefully designed field experiments can test specific anti-poverty policies, and made evidence on what works central to development debates.","context":"Duflo was born in Paris and studied history and economics in France before completing her doctorate at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, where she later became a professor. Her early research used natural experiments, such as a large school construction programme in Indonesia, to estimate the effects of education. In development economics at the time, many policies were adopted with little reliable evidence on their effects. With Abhijit Banerjee and Sendhil Mullainathan she founded J-PAL in 2003, aiming to apply randomised evaluation, a method borrowed from medical research, to questions about education, health, finance and governance in low-income countries.","ideas":[{"h":"Field experiments in policy","p":"Duflo argued that development policy can be broken down into many specific questions, each of which can be tested. Should mosquito nets be given free or sold at a subsidised price? Does adding teachers improve learning? Randomised evaluation compares groups that differ only in whether they received the policy, so differences in outcomes can be attributed to the policy itself."},{"h":"The economist as plumber","p":"Duflo has argued that economists should attend to the details of how policies are implemented, like a plumber fixing leaks in a system. A well-designed programme on paper can fail because of small practical problems, such as unclear rules, missing information or weak incentives for staff. Testing and adjusting these details can make a large difference."},{"h":"Women’s empowerment and development","p":"Duflo examined how development and the position of women interact. Her research on political reservations for women in Indian village councils found that women leaders tended to invest more in public goods valued by women. She argued that economic development and empowerment support each other, but that development alone may not remove gender inequality without targeted policy."},{"h":"Behaviour and small nudges","p":"Many of Duflo’s experiments show that small changes in timing, convenience or framing can affect choices such as fertiliser use, vaccination or saving. For example, offering farmers a chance to buy fertiliser at harvest time, when they have cash, can raise its use. This links development economics with behavioural economics and choice architecture. In the IB course, choice architecture and nudges are HL only (2.4 and 2.7)."}],"contribution":"Duflo helped make randomised evaluation a standard method in development economics and in policy evaluation more widely. Her work demonstrated that rigorous evidence can be gathered on specific policies in difficult settings, and that implementation details matter as much as broad policy direction. She also brought gender and political economy into experimental research, showing how institutions shape who benefits from public spending.","assumptions":["Random assignment allows causal effects to be identified","Specific policies can be isolated and tested separately","Behavioural tendencies such as procrastination affect decisions in predictable ways","Evidence from well-designed trials can improve policy if it is acted upon"],"influence":"Governments, charities and international agencies increasingly fund and use randomised evaluations, and J-PAL works with policymakers in many countries. Duflo’s findings on subsidised health products, teaching methods and gender quotas have informed policy debates. She was the second woman to receive the economics prize. Her work, with Banerjee’s, has also shaped the way economists communicate research to general readers.","criticisms":["Angus Deaton and Nancy Cartwright argued that randomisation does not by itself ensure that a result will hold in a different place or at a larger scale.","Some critics argue that focusing on small, testable interventions can neglect the macroeconomic and institutional changes that drove growth in economies that became rich.","Programme effects found in a trial may change when governments scale them up, because implementation, prices or behaviour may differ."],"modern":"Duflo’s work is relevant to IB subtopic 4.10 on development strategies and to 1.2 on how economists use evidence. Discussions about cash transfers, women’s empowerment, education policy and health subsidies regularly cite experimental findings. Students can use her approach to evaluate a development policy by asking what specific mechanism it relies on, how its effect could be measured, and whether evidence from elsewhere is likely to apply.","works":[{"t":"Poor Economics (with Abhijit Banerjee)","y":2011}],"honours":"Nobel memorial prize in economic sciences, 2019, shared with Abhijit Banerjee and Michael Kremer","misread":"Students sometimes think that randomised trials can settle any economic question. Duflo uses them for specific, testable policy questions; many important questions, such as the effects of trade or monetary policy, cannot be randomised and need other methods.","subs":["4.10","1.2","2.7"],"kc":["Equity","Economic well-being","Intervention"],"terms":["Nudge","Choice architecture","Behavioural economics","Human capital","Economic development"],"dg":[],"question":"How might you design a fair test of whether a particular government subsidy changes behaviour in a community you know, and what would limit the results?","connects":[{"id":"banerjee","why":"Her closest collaborator, co-founder of J-PAL and co-author of Poor Economics."},{"id":"sen","why":"Her work on women’s position in development connects with Sen’s concern with gender inequality."},{"id":"acemoglu","why":"Acemoglu’s institutional approach poses larger-scale questions that experiments cannot easily test."}]},{"id":"acemoglu","name":"Daron Acemoglu","life":"born 1967","era":"Institutional economics and political economy","headline":"Acemoglu, with Simon Johnson and James Robinson, argued that differences in political and economic institutions are a fundamental cause of why some nations are rich and others poor.","context":"Acemoglu was born in Istanbul, studied in Britain and has spent his career at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. By the 1990s, growth economics had established that physical capital, education and technology are close causes of income differences, but not why some societies accumulate them and others do not. Explanations based on geography, culture or policy competed. Acemoglu and his co-authors argued that institutions, meaning the rules that shape incentives in an economy, are more fundamental. The challenge was to show causation rather than correlation, since rich countries might simply be able to afford better institutions.","ideas":[{"h":"Colonial origins","p":"In a 2001 paper on the colonial origins of comparative development, Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson argued that where European colonisers faced high mortality, they set up extractive institutions designed to transfer resources. Where they could settle, they built institutions protecting property and limiting state power. These institutions persisted. Using settler mortality as a source of variation in institutions not caused by current income, they estimated a large effect of institutions on income today."},{"h":"Inclusive and extractive institutions","p":"In Why Nations Fail, Acemoglu and Robinson distinguish inclusive economic institutions, which secure property rights, enforce contracts and open markets to new entrants, from extractive ones, which concentrate gains in a narrow elite. Inclusive economic institutions depend on inclusive political institutions that distribute power widely. Extractive systems can grow for a time but tend to stall because elites block innovation that threatens them."},{"h":"Critical junctures","p":"Small institutional differences can widen when major shocks, such as plagues, colonisation or new trade opportunities, interact with existing institutions. These critical junctures can push societies onto different paths. Outcomes therefore depend on history and contingency, not fixed geography or culture. The Black Death, for example, is argued to have weakened serfdom in western Europe while it was strengthened in parts of eastern Europe, as landlords there responded differently to labour shortages."},{"h":"Technology and labour","p":"In more recent work, Acemoglu has studied how automation and artificial intelligence affect work. He argues that the direction of technological change is a choice, shaped by institutions and incentives, and that technologies which replace workers without creating new tasks can lower labour’s share of income. Policy, in his view, can steer innovation towards uses that complement workers."}],"contribution":"Acemoglu and his co-authors made institutions central to explaining long-run growth and development, and set a demanding standard for identifying causal effects at the scale of whole economies. Their framework links political power to economic incentives, so that the question of why nations differ in income becomes partly a question of who controls the rules. This approach shaped development economics, economic history and political economy.","assumptions":["Institutions shape incentives to invest, innovate and work","Those in power choose institutions that serve their interests","Institutions persist over long periods unless disrupted","Historical variation, such as settler mortality, can isolate the effect of institutions from that of income"],"influence":"The institutional view influenced the agendas of development agencies, which gave more attention to governance, rule of law and property rights from the late 1990s. Why Nations Fail reached a wide audience and is often cited in debates about corruption, state capacity and political reform. Acemoglu’s work on automation has informed policy debates about artificial intelligence and labour markets.","criticisms":["David Albouy questioned the reliability of the settler mortality data used in the 2001 paper, which the authors disputed.","Edward Glaeser and co-authors argued that human capital may be a more fundamental cause of growth, with institutions improving as countries become richer and more educated.","Critics argue that the inclusive–extractive distinction is broad, and that cases such as rapid growth under authoritarian rule are hard to classify or explain within it.","Geographical explanations, associated with Jeffrey Sachs and others, argue that disease burden and location have direct effects not captured by institutions."],"modern":"The institutional approach underlies much of the discussion in IB subtopics 4.9 and 4.10 about weak institutions, corruption and the role of the state in development. It encourages students to ask who benefits from existing rules and why reforms may be resisted. His recent work on technology connects to debates about the future of work, inequality and the labour share in both rich and developing economies.","works":[{"t":"Why Nations Fail (with James Robinson)","y":2012}],"honours":"Nobel memorial prize in economic sciences, 2024, shared with Simon Johnson and James Robinson","misread":"Students sometimes read Acemoglu as arguing that institutions are permanently fixed by history. His framework stresses that institutions persist but can change at critical junctures, and that reform is possible though often resisted by those who gain from the existing system.","subs":["4.9","4.10","3.4"],"kc":["Change","Economic well-being","Equity"],"terms":["Property rights","Economic growth","Economic development","Human capital","Real GDP per capita"],"dg":["growth"],"question":"Using the distinction between inclusive and extractive institutions, how far can institutions explain the growth record of an economy you have studied?","connects":[{"id":"ostrom","why":"Both place institutions and rules at the centre of economic outcomes."},{"id":"banerjee","why":"Their focus on testing specific programmes contrasts with his emphasis on whole-society institutions."},{"id":"smith","why":"Smith also linked prosperity to secure property, justice and the absence of monopoly privilege."}]}],"causal":[{"id":"inflation","q":"Why did inflation rise?","unit":3,"subs":["3.2","3.3","3.5"],"kc":["Change","Economic well-being","Intervention"],"dg":"adas","dg2":"costpush","event":"A country’s consumer price index rises faster than in previous years. The increase is broad: it shows up in many categories of goods and services, not only in one volatile item. Households report that their incomes buy less, and firms report rising prices for both what they sell and what they buy as inputs.","initial":"Two facts decide which explanation fits. First, how close the economy was to full capacity: low unemployment and high capacity use make demand-pull more likely. Second, whether input costs such as energy, imported materials or wages had jumped. Expectations matter too: if people expect inflation to persist, they may build it into wage claims and prices.","mechanism":["Route A (demand-pull): consumption, investment, government spending or net exports rise → AD shifts right while the economy is near full capacity.","Firms cannot raise output much without paying more for scarce labour and capacity → the economy moves up a steepening SRAS curve, so the price level rises (a movement along SRAS).","Route B (cost-push): a rise in energy, raw-material or wage costs raises firms’ unit costs → SRAS shifts left.","With higher costs, firms raise prices and cut output → the price level rises while real GDP falls, a combination often called stagflation.","Either route can be reinforced if workers and firms come to expect higher inflation and raise wages and prices in advance, shifting SRAS left again.","Decision test: if output and employment rose alongside prices, demand-pull fits better; if output fell or stalled while prices rose, cost-push fits better."],"agents":[{"who":"Households","what":"Spend more if incomes, credit or confidence rise (demand-pull); lose purchasing power if their wages lag behind prices."},{"who":"Firms","what":"Raise prices when demand outruns capacity, or pass on higher input costs to protect profit margins."},{"who":"Workers and unions","what":"Seek higher money wages to protect real wages, which can feed a wage-price spiral if expectations shift."},{"who":"Central bank","what":"Judges whether the rise is temporary or persistent and decides whether to tighten monetary policy."},{"who":"Foreign suppliers","what":"Higher world prices for energy or materials raise import costs and transmit cost-push pressure."}],"diagram":"Draw two AD–AS panels. Demand-pull: shift AD right from AD₁ to AD₂ near full capacity; the price level rises from P₁ to P₂ and real GDP rises from Y₁ to Y₂. Cost-push: shift SRAS left from SRAS₁ to SRAS₂; the price level rises and real GDP falls. Label which panel matches the observed data on output.","variables":[{"v":"Average price level","dir":"up","why":"Rises under both routes; the cause differs, so the price level alone cannot identify the route."},{"v":"Real GDP","dir":"ambiguous","why":"Rises under demand-pull, but falls under cost-push as higher costs lead firms to cut output."},{"v":"Unemployment","dir":"ambiguous","why":"Tends to fall with demand-pull and rise with cost-push, because output moves in opposite directions."},{"v":"Real wages","dir":"down","why":"Usually fall at first, because money wages tend to adjust to higher prices with a lag."},{"v":"Inflation expectations","dir":"up","why":"May rise if inflation persists, making future wage and price setting more inflationary."}],"short":"In the short run, demand-pull inflation comes with stronger output and falling unemployment, so the main costs are lost purchasing power and uncertainty. Cost-push inflation is harder to bear: prices rise while output and jobs fall. Lower-income households, who spend a larger share of income on food and energy, may lose most. Savers holding cash lose if interest rates stay below inflation.","long":"In the longer run, a one-off cost shock may fade once input prices stop rising, so inflation can fall back without policy action. Demand-pull inflation tends to persist if spending keeps outrunning capacity. The greatest risk under either route is that expectations become unanchored: wages and prices then rise because people expect them to, and bringing inflation down may need tighter policy and slower growth.","evidence":"Supporting evidence for demand-pull would include rising real GDP, low unemployment, high capacity use and fast growth in spending or credit. For cost-push, look for rising energy, import or wage costs that came before the price rise, and for weak output. Studies of recent episodes generally find that both routes operated together, with supply bottlenecks and energy prices interacting with recovering demand. Evidence on how much each contributed is mixed.","cases":["MAC-016","MAC-039","MAC-042"],"evaluation":["It depends on where the economy was on its AS curve: near full capacity, extra demand mostly raises prices.","In the short run, cost-push inflation creates a policy dilemma, because raising interest rates to curb prices may deepen the fall in output.","In the long run, inflation tends to persist only if expectations or continued demand growth sustain it.","A key limitation is that real episodes usually mix both routes, so a clean demand-pull or cost-push label may oversimplify.","Stakeholders may experience inflation unequally: borrowers with fixed-rate debt may gain, while savers and those on fixed incomes lose."],"alternatives":[{"h":"A depreciation of the currency","p":"A weaker currency raises the domestic price of imported goods and inputs. This works like a cost-push shock, but its source is the foreign exchange market rather than domestic costs. It is more likely to matter in an economy that imports a large share of its energy, food or components.","test":"Check whether the exchange rate fell before prices rose, and whether import prices rose faster than domestic service prices."},{"h":"A change in indirect taxes or administered prices","p":"A rise in VAT, excise duties or regulated energy tariffs lifts measured prices in one step. This raises the inflation rate for about a year but is not a continuing process, so it may not justify a policy response.","test":"Look for a jump in the index at the date of the tax change, and compare headline inflation with measures that exclude taxes."}],"hl":null,"next":["ratehike","currency"]},{"id":"unemployment","q":"Why did unemployment rise?","unit":3,"subs":["3.2","3.3","3.7"],"kc":["Change","Economic well-being","Equity"],"dg":"adas","dg2":"cycle","event":"The unemployment rate rises over several quarters. More people who are actively seeking work cannot find a job. The rise may be spread across the whole economy, or concentrated in particular industries, regions or skill groups, and that pattern is a first clue to its cause.","initial":"What matters is the state of aggregate demand, the structure of the economy and how flexible the labour market is. If spending has fallen across the board, cyclical unemployment is likely. If technology, trade or consumer tastes have shifted demand away from some industries, structural unemployment is likely. Skills, mobility and information about vacancies affect how quickly workers can move.","mechanism":["Cyclical route: a fall in consumption, investment or exports shifts AD left → real GDP falls below potential output.","Firms need fewer workers to produce less output → labour is a derived demand, so employment falls and cyclical (demand-deficient) unemployment rises.","Structural route: technological change, trade competition or shifting tastes reduce demand for particular skills or industries, even if total AD is steady.","Workers displaced from declining sectors lack the skills, location or information to fill vacancies elsewhere → a mismatch persists even with unfilled jobs.","Decision test: cyclical unemployment comes with falling output and few vacancies; structural unemployment can coexist with many vacancies in other sectors or regions."],"agents":[{"who":"Firms","what":"Cut hiring and lay off workers when sales fall, or replace some tasks with new technology."},{"who":"Households","what":"Cut spending when jobs feel less secure, which can deepen a cyclical downturn."},{"who":"Displaced workers","what":"Search for new jobs; some need retraining or must move to find work."},{"who":"Government","what":"Pays unemployment benefits and may use demand-side or supply-side policies, depending on the diagnosis."}],"diagram":"On an AD–AS diagram, shift AD left from AD₁ to AD₂; real GDP falls from Y₁ to Y₂, below full-employment output Yf, so a deflationary (recessionary) gap opens. Mark the gap and link it to cyclical unemployment. Structural unemployment is not shown by an AD shift; note it separately as part of the natural rate at Yf.","variables":[{"v":"Real GDP","dir":"ambiguous","why":"Falls in a cyclical downturn; may be little changed if the rise in unemployment is mainly structural."},{"v":"Job vacancies","dir":"ambiguous","why":"Fall in a recession; may stay high or rise in growing sectors when unemployment is structural."},{"v":"Household consumption","dir":"down","why":"Lower incomes and weaker confidence lead households to spend less, especially on non-essential goods."},{"v":"Government budget balance","dir":"down","why":"Tax revenue falls and benefit spending rises, so the budget deficit tends to widen (automatic stabilisers, HL only)."},{"v":"Inflationary pressure","dir":"down","why":"Spare capacity and weaker wage bargaining usually reduce pressure on prices in a cyclical downturn."}],"short":"In the short run, cyclical unemployment lowers household incomes and spending, which can spread the downturn to other sectors. Government borrowing rises as tax revenue falls and benefit payments rise. Structural unemployment may hit specific towns or groups hard while the wider economy looks healthy. Either way, the costs include lost output, lower incomes and greater stress for affected households.","long":"In the longer run, cyclical unemployment usually falls as demand recovers, though recovery can be slow. If people stay unemployed for long periods, their skills may fade and employers may become reluctant to hire them, so some cyclical unemployment can turn into longer-term structural unemployment. Structural unemployment tends to fall only as workers retrain, move or as new industries grow.","evidence":"Supporting evidence for a cyclical cause would include falling real GDP, falling vacancies and job losses spread across many sectors. For a structural cause, look for rising unemployment in particular industries or regions alongside vacancies elsewhere, and long durations of unemployment. Studies generally find that deep recessions can leave lasting scars on some workers, but the size of this effect varies across countries and labour-market institutions.","cases":["MAC-032","MAC-017","MAC-040"],"evaluation":["It depends on whether job losses are spread across the economy or concentrated in particular sectors, which points to cyclical or structural causes.","In the short run, demand-side policy may reduce cyclical unemployment but does little to fix a skills mismatch.","In the long run, structural unemployment may need retraining, mobility support or education reform, which take time to work.","This may be stronger if long spells of unemployment erode skills, turning a cyclical problem into a structural one.","Stakeholders may experience unemployment very unequally: young people, low-skilled workers and particular regions often bear more of the cost."],"alternatives":[{"h":"Frictional unemployment from more people entering or changing jobs","p":"If more people enter the labour force, for example graduates or returning carers, measured unemployment can rise while they search, even if employment is also rising. This is usually short term and is not a sign of weak demand, so it calls for better job-matching rather than a demand-side stimulus.","test":"Check whether employment and the participation rate rose too, and whether most of the unemployed have short search durations."},{"h":"A change in measurement","p":"A change in survey method or in how ‘actively seeking work’ is defined can shift the measured rate without any change in the real labour market. Comparisons across years, or between countries that measure unemployment differently, may then mislead students into seeing a cause where there is none.","test":"Look for a break in the data series and compare it with other indicators such as employment counts or benefit claims."}],"hl":null,"next":["stimulus","supplyside"]},{"id":"growth","q":"Why did economic growth slow?","unit":3,"subs":["3.1","3.2","3.7","4.9"],"kc":["Change","Economic well-being","Sustainability"],"dg":"adas","dg2":"growth","event":"The annual rate of growth of real GDP falls compared with earlier years. Output may still be rising, but more slowly. A slowdown is different from a recession: real GDP need not fall. The question is whether demand has weakened for a while or whether the economy’s capacity to produce is growing more slowly.","initial":"The key starting conditions are the size of the output gap, the growth of the labour force, the rate of investment and productivity growth, and the economy’s stage of development. A fast-growing economy catching up with richer ones may see growth slow as easy gains run out. Debt levels, confidence and the state of export markets also matter.","mechanism":["Demand-side route: weaker consumption, investment or exports shift AD left or slow its growth → actual growth falls below the growth of potential output.","Firms facing weaker sales postpone investment → this lowers AD now and also slows the future growth of the capital stock.","Supply-side route: slower growth in the labour force, capital or productivity slows the rightward shift of LRAS → potential growth itself falls.","In a catching-up economy, moving workers from farming into industry and copying existing technology yields large gains at first, but these gains tend to shrink over time.","Decision test: a demand-side slowdown shows spare capacity and falling inflation; a supply-side slowdown may show little spare capacity, with inflation steady or rising."],"agents":[{"who":"Firms","what":"Delay investment when expected sales or confidence fall, slowing both demand and capacity growth."},{"who":"Households","what":"Save more and spend less if incomes are uncertain or debts are high."},{"who":"Government","what":"Chooses between demand-side support and longer-term supply-side reform depending on the diagnosis."},{"who":"Foreign buyers","what":"Buy fewer exports if trading partners slow down, reducing AD."}],"diagram":"For a demand-side slowdown, draw AD shifting right by less than LRAS, or shifting left, so real GDP falls below potential and a deflationary gap opens. For a supply-side slowdown, use the long-run growth diagram: LRAS still shifts right, but by a smaller distance than before, so potential output grows more slowly.","variables":[{"v":"Real GDP growth rate","dir":"down","why":"The observed change; output may still rise, but by a smaller percentage each year."},{"v":"Investment","dir":"down","why":"Weaker expected demand or confidence leads firms to postpone spending on capital goods."},{"v":"Unemployment","dir":"ambiguous","why":"Tends to rise if the slowdown is demand-led; may change little if the labour force is also growing more slowly."},{"v":"Inflation","dir":"ambiguous","why":"Tends to fall if demand weakens; may hold steady if potential output growth slowed as well."},{"v":"Tax revenue","dir":"down","why":"Slower growth in incomes and spending slows the growth of tax receipts."}],"short":"In the short run, slower growth tends to mean weaker hiring, slower rises in living standards and lower business confidence. If demand is the cause, spare capacity appears and inflation may ease. Tax revenue grows more slowly, which can widen the budget deficit. Firms that depend on rising sales, such as construction and durable goods producers, are often hit first.","long":"In the longer run, a demand-led slowdown may reverse as confidence and spending recover. A supply-side slowdown is more persistent: lower investment, an ageing population or slower productivity growth reduce potential growth for years. Small differences in annual growth compound into large differences in living standards over a decade, so a lasting slowdown matters more than a temporary one.","evidence":"Evidence for a demand-side cause would include falling capacity use, rising unemployment, easing inflation and weak spending or credit. Evidence for a supply-side cause would include slower growth in the labour force or in output per worker, with little spare capacity. Studies of catching-up economies generally find that growth tends to slow as income per head rises, though the timing and size of the slowdown vary widely.","cases":["MAC-010","MAC-045","MAC-008"],"evaluation":["It depends on whether the slowdown reflects weaker demand or slower growth in productive capacity, because the policy responses differ.","In the short run, demand-side policy may close an output gap, but it cannot raise potential growth.","In the long run, growth depends mainly on investment, skills, technology and institutions.","A key limitation is that potential output cannot be observed directly, so economists may disagree about how much of a slowdown is cyclical.","Stakeholders may experience a slowdown unevenly: new entrants to the labour market and firms in cyclical industries tend to be hit first."],"alternatives":[{"h":"An external shock in trading partners","p":"If major export markets slow, demand for the country’s exports falls even if domestic conditions are sound. Open economies with a high share of exports in GDP are most exposed. The slowdown is still demand-led, but the policy response differs, because domestic stimulus cannot restore foreign demand directly.","test":"Compare the timing of the slowdown with trading partners’ growth and check whether net exports fell more than domestic spending."},{"h":"A one-off supply disruption","p":"A drought, pandemic or energy shortage can cut output for a period without changing the economy’s long-run capacity. Growth may rebound once the disruption passes, so treating it as a lasting fall in potential growth, or responding with long-term reforms, could be a misdiagnosis.","test":"Look for a sharp fall concentrated in the affected sectors and a recovery once the disruption ends."}],"hl":null,"next":["supplyside","unemployment"]},{"id":"currency","q":"Why did the currency depreciate?","unit":4,"subs":["4.5","4.6","3.5"],"kc":["Interdependence","Change","Intervention"],"dg":"fx","dg2":null,"event":"Under a floating exchange rate, the value of a country’s currency falls against other major currencies over several months. Each unit of domestic currency now buys fewer units of foreign currency. Import prices in domestic currency start to rise, and exporters find their goods cheaper for foreign buyers.","initial":"The relevant conditions include the gap between domestic and foreign interest rates, the inflation rate relative to trading partners, the size of the current account deficit, dependence on short-term capital inflows, and investor confidence in the government and central bank. Whether the currency floats freely or is managed matters too.","mechanism":["Foreign interest rates rise relative to domestic rates, or confidence falls → investors want fewer domestic financial assets, so demand for the currency falls (D shifts left).","Residents buy more foreign assets or imports → they supply more domestic currency to buy foreign currency (S shifts right).","Higher domestic inflation than abroad makes exports less competitive and imports more attractive → demand for the currency falls and supply rises over time.","With demand lower and/or supply higher, the equilibrium exchange rate falls → a depreciation, shown as a new equilibrium with a lower price of the currency.","Expectations can reinforce the fall: if traders expect further depreciation, they sell the currency now, which brings the fall forward."],"agents":[{"who":"Foreign investors","what":"Move funds to countries with higher returns or lower risk, reducing demand for the currency."},{"who":"Domestic households and firms","what":"Supply currency when they buy imports or foreign assets; importers face higher costs after the fall."},{"who":"Exporters","what":"Find their goods more price-competitive abroad, which may raise export volumes over time."},{"who":"Central bank","what":"May raise interest rates or sell foreign reserves to slow the fall, or allow it to continue."}],"diagram":"Draw the foreign exchange market for the domestic currency, with the exchange rate (foreign currency per unit) on the vertical axis. Shift demand for the currency left from D₁ to D₂, and if relevant supply right from S₁ to S₂. The equilibrium exchange rate falls from e₁ to e₂. The quantity traded may rise or fall.","variables":[{"v":"Exchange rate","dir":"down","why":"The observed change: fewer units of foreign currency per unit of domestic currency."},{"v":"Import prices in domestic currency","dir":"up","why":"Each unit of foreign currency costs more domestic currency, so imported goods and inputs become dearer."},{"v":"Export competitiveness","dir":"up","why":"Exports become cheaper in foreign-currency terms, which may raise demand for them."},{"v":"Inflation","dir":"up","why":"Dearer imports raise costs and consumer prices, especially where imports are a large share of spending."},{"v":"Current account balance","dir":"ambiguous","why":"Depends on how strongly import and export volumes respond to the new prices, and how quickly."}],"short":"In the short run, the depreciation raises the domestic price of imports. Consumers pay more for imported goods, and firms that rely on imported inputs face higher costs. Exporters gain price competitiveness, but export volumes may take time to respond, because contracts and buying habits adjust slowly. Inflation may rise, which can put pressure on the central bank to respond.","long":"In the longer run, export volumes may rise and import volumes fall as buyers switch, which can improve the current account. However, if the depreciation feeds into higher wages and prices, the competitive gain may be eroded. Firms and governments with debts in foreign currency face a heavier burden. Whether the depreciation helps or harms overall depends on how the economy adjusts.","evidence":"Supporting evidence would include a widening gap between foreign and domestic interest rates, capital outflows in the financial account, higher relative inflation or a worsening current account before the fall. Studies generally find that interest-rate differentials and shifts in investor confidence explain much short-run movement, while relative inflation matters more over longer periods. Exchange rates are hard to forecast, so explanations are often made after the event.","cases":["GLO-045","MAC-029","GLO-022"],"evaluation":["It depends on what caused the fall: a confidence crisis has different implications from a gradual adjustment to higher relative inflation.","In the short run, the trade balance may worsen before it improves, because import and export volumes respond slowly (the J-curve, HL only).","In the long run, the gain in competitiveness may be eroded if the depreciation raises domestic wages and prices.","This may be stronger if the economy has a large current account deficit financed by short-term capital inflows.","Stakeholders may experience the depreciation differently: exporters and tourism firms may gain, while importers, consumers and foreign-currency borrowers lose."],"alternatives":[{"h":"A deliberate policy choice","p":"A central bank may cut interest rates or sell domestic currency to support exports or growth. The currency then falls because of a policy decision, not a loss of confidence, and the authorities may see the depreciation as a desired outcome rather than a problem to be fixed.","test":"Check whether the fall followed an interest-rate cut or announced intervention, rather than capital flight or rising risk premiums."},{"h":"Strength of the foreign currency rather than weakness at home","p":"If a major currency rises against almost all others, the domestic currency appears to depreciate even though nothing has changed at home. The cause then lies abroad, for example in higher foreign interest rates or a rush into a safe-haven currency, and domestic policy may not need to respond.","test":"Compare the currency against several other currencies, or a trade-weighted index, rather than against one currency alone."}],"hl":"HL: the J-curve and the Marshall–Lerner condition (why the current account may worsen before it improves)","next":["inflation","ratehike"]},{"id":"ratehike","q":"Why did the central bank raise interest rates, and what happened next?","unit":3,"subs":["3.5","3.3","4.5"],"kc":["Intervention","Change","Economic well-being"],"dg":"adas","dg2":"fx","event":"A central bank raises its policy interest rate, sometimes in several steps over a year. Commercial banks soon raise the rates they charge on loans and mortgages, and many offer higher rates on savings. Commentators debate whether the rise will bring inflation down without causing a recession.","initial":"The usual starting point is inflation above the central bank’s target, often with low unemployment and strong demand. What happens next depends on how much household and business debt is on variable rates, how credible the central bank is, whether inflation is mainly demand-pull or cost-push, and how open the economy is to capital flows.","mechanism":["Inflation is above target and expected to stay there → the central bank raises the policy rate to reduce aggregate demand.","Banks raise borrowing costs → borrowing for houses, cars and investment becomes more expensive, so consumption and investment fall; saving becomes more attractive.","Households with variable-rate mortgages have less disposable income → consumption falls further, and AD shifts left.","Higher interest rates attract capital inflows → demand for the currency rises and it appreciates, making exports dearer and imports cheaper, so net exports fall.","AD shifts left → real GDP grows more slowly and inflationary pressure eases, with a time lag that can be long."],"agents":[{"who":"Central bank","what":"Raises the policy rate to bring inflation back towards its target and to keep expectations anchored."},{"who":"Households","what":"Borrowers pay more interest and spend less; savers earn more."},{"who":"Firms","what":"Postpone investment projects as borrowing costs rise and expected demand weakens."},{"who":"Foreign investors","what":"Move funds into domestic assets for the higher return, pushing the currency up."},{"who":"Government","what":"Pays more interest on new borrowing, which can strain the budget."}],"diagram":"Start with an AD–AS diagram showing an inflationary gap, with equilibrium beyond full-employment output Yf. Shift AD left from AD₁ to AD₂; the price level falls relative to its previous path and real GDP moves back towards Yf. A second diagram can show the foreign exchange market, with higher demand for the currency raising the exchange rate.","variables":[{"v":"Consumption and investment","dir":"down","why":"Borrowing becomes more expensive and saving more attractive, so interest-sensitive spending falls."},{"v":"Inflation","dir":"down","why":"Weaker AD reduces demand-pull pressure, usually after a lag of many months."},{"v":"Real GDP growth","dir":"down","why":"Lower AD slows output growth; output can fall if rates rise sharply."},{"v":"Exchange rate","dir":"up","why":"Higher returns attract capital inflows, increasing demand for the currency."},{"v":"Unemployment","dir":"up","why":"Slower growth in demand tends to reduce hiring, so unemployment may rise."}],"short":"In the short run, borrowers face higher repayments and cut spending, and housing markets often cool first. Firms delay investment. The currency may appreciate, putting pressure on exporters. Inflation may not fall at once, because policy works with a lag. If inflation was driven by a supply shock, higher rates may reduce output without doing much about its cause.","long":"In the longer run, if the rise is credible, inflation expectations stay anchored and inflation returns towards target, after which rates can be lowered. The cost may be a period of slower growth and higher unemployment. If rates are raised too far or too late, a recession is possible. Lower investment today may also reduce the growth of productive capacity.","evidence":"Supporting evidence would include slowing credit growth, falling house prices or mortgage approvals, weaker investment and an easing in inflation after a lag. Studies generally find that monetary policy affects inflation with long and variable lags, and that the effect is stronger when more debt is on variable rates. Evidence is mixed on how well higher rates work against inflation that comes mainly from supply shocks.","cases":["MAC-012","MAC-029"],"evaluation":["It depends on the cause of inflation: higher rates reduce demand-pull pressure more directly than cost-push pressure.","In the short run, time lags mean that inflation may keep rising for some months after rates go up.","In the long run, credibility matters: if people believe the central bank will hit its target, smaller rate rises may be needed.","This may be weaker if most borrowing is on fixed rates, or if households and firms hold large cash savings.","There may be unintended consequences: higher debt-servicing costs can strain indebted households, firms and governments, and the currency rise can hurt exporters."],"alternatives":[{"h":"Defending the currency","p":"A central bank may raise rates mainly to stop a sharp depreciation that threatens to raise import prices and destabilise the economy, rather than because domestic demand is too strong. This is more common in economies that import much of their food and energy or that borrow heavily in foreign currencies.","test":"Check whether the rise followed a fall in the exchange rate or capital outflows, rather than strong domestic spending."},{"h":"Following other central banks","p":"If major economies raise rates, a smaller open economy may follow to avoid capital outflows and depreciation, even if its own demand is weak. Its policy is then shaped by interdependence with larger economies, and the rate rise may slow its economy more than domestic conditions alone would justify.","test":"Compare the timing with rate decisions abroad and with the central bank’s own statements about its reasons."}],"hl":null,"next":["inflation","currency"]},{"id":"stimulus","q":"Why did the government increase spending in a recession, and what happened next?","unit":3,"subs":["3.6","3.2","3.3"],"kc":["Intervention","Economic well-being","Change"],"dg":"defgap","dg2":"multiplier","event":"During a recession, a government announces higher spending on infrastructure, public services or transfers, and allows its budget deficit to widen. Supporters say it will protect jobs and incomes; critics worry about rising public debt and whether the money will be spent in time to make a difference.","initial":"The economy has a deflationary gap: real GDP is below potential and unemployment is high. Households and firms are cautious, so private spending is weak. What follows depends on how much spare capacity exists, how much of extra income is spent rather than saved or used on imports, the state of public finances and whether interest rates are already low.","mechanism":["Private spending falls and a deflationary gap opens → the government raises its own spending (G), a component of AD.","Higher G shifts AD right → firms receiving government orders raise output and hire workers.","Those workers and firms spend part of their extra income → further rounds of spending raise AD again, so the final rise in real GDP can exceed the initial injection (the multiplier).","With spare capacity, firms can raise output without large cost increases → real GDP rises with only a small rise in the price level, a movement along the flatter part of the AS curve.","Government borrowing rises → if this pushes up interest rates, some private investment may be discouraged (crowding out), reducing the net effect."],"agents":[{"who":"Government","what":"Raises spending and borrowing to support demand, accepting a larger deficit for a period."},{"who":"Firms","what":"Receive orders, raise output and may rehire workers; may invest more if confidence improves."},{"who":"Households","what":"Spend part of any extra income, and save more if they expect future tax rises or remain anxious."},{"who":"Lenders and bond markets","what":"Buy government debt; may demand higher interest if they doubt the public finances."}],"diagram":"Draw an AD–AS diagram with equilibrium at Y₁, below full-employment output Yf, showing a deflationary gap. Shift AD right from AD₁ to AD₂; real GDP rises towards Yf and the price level rises only slightly because there is spare capacity. Mark the reduction in the gap. A multiplier diagram can show the successive rounds of spending.","variables":[{"v":"Real GDP","dir":"up","why":"Higher government spending raises AD; with spare capacity, most of the effect falls on output rather than prices."},{"v":"Unemployment","dir":"down","why":"Firms need more workers to meet higher demand, so cyclical unemployment tends to fall."},{"v":"Budget deficit","dir":"up","why":"Spending rises immediately; extra tax revenue from recovery offsets only part of it."},{"v":"Price level","dir":"up","why":"Rises slightly with spare capacity; would rise more if the economy were close to full capacity."},{"v":"Interest rates","dir":"ambiguous","why":"May rise if government borrowing competes for funds; may stay low if private demand for loans is weak."}],"short":"In the short run, if the spending is timely and well targeted, output and employment rise and the fall in incomes is limited. The multiplier may be larger in a deep recession, because spare capacity is high and the central bank may hold interest rates down. Time lags in planning and approving projects can delay the effect, sometimes until recovery has already begun.","long":"In the longer run, the spending leaves higher public debt that must be serviced. If the spending built useful infrastructure or skills, it may also raise potential output. If it is not withdrawn as the economy recovers, it may add to inflationary pressure. Some argue that expected future tax rises lead households to save more, weakening the effect.","evidence":"Supporting evidence would include a rise in output and employment in sectors that received spending, and a narrowing output gap after the policy began. Studies generally find that fiscal multipliers vary widely: they tend to be larger in recessions, when interest rates are low and in less open economies, and smaller when public debt is high. Separating the policy’s effect from a natural recovery is difficult, so estimates are uncertain.","cases":["MAC-007","MAC-015","MAC-049"],"evaluation":["It depends on the size of the multiplier, which is larger when spare capacity is high and less extra income leaks into saving, taxes and imports.","In the short run, time lags may mean that spending arrives after the recovery has begun.","In the long run, higher public debt raises interest costs and may limit future fiscal choices.","This may be weaker if government borrowing pushes up interest rates and crowds out private investment.","There may be unintended consequences: spending that is hard to reverse may add to inflation once the economy is back near full capacity."],"alternatives":[{"h":"Automatic stabilisers rather than new policy","p":"In a recession, benefit payments rise and tax receipts fall without any new decision. The deficit widens and demand is supported, even though the government did not choose to spend more. Part of the observed rise in spending and borrowing may therefore not be a deliberate stimulus at all.","test":"Separate discretionary measures announced in the budget from changes in spending and revenue that follow from the cycle."},{"h":"A natural recovery","p":"Recessions often end as firms rebuild stocks and confidence returns. A rise in output after the stimulus may partly reflect this, not the policy itself. Assuming that whatever follows a policy was caused by it confuses correlation with causation.","test":"Compare sectors or regions that received more spending with similar ones that received less, looking at timing."}],"hl":"HL: the Keynesian multiplier, crowding out and automatic stabilisers","next":["ratehike","unemployment"]},{"id":"tax","q":"Why did the price of a taxed good rise by less than the tax?","unit":2,"subs":["2.7","2.5","2.6"],"kc":["Intervention","Equity","Efficiency"],"dg":"tax","dg2":"ped","event":"A government places a specific (per-unit) indirect tax on a product. After the tax, the price consumers pay rises, but by less than the full amount of the tax per unit. Producers keep less per unit sold than before. Quantity bought and sold falls.","initial":"The key conditions are the price elasticities of demand and supply. If consumers can easily switch to substitutes, demand is relatively elastic. If producers can easily reduce output or switch to other products, supply is relatively elastic. The market is assumed to be competitive, with no other changes to demand or costs during the period.","mechanism":["The tax raises firms’ costs per unit sold → the supply curve shifts vertically upwards by the amount of the tax (S to S + tax).","At the original price, firms now supply less → excess demand pushes the price up, a movement along the demand curve to a new equilibrium with a higher price and lower quantity.","Consumers reduce the quantity they buy as the price rises → firms cannot pass on the whole tax without losing sales.","The tax per unit is split: the rise in the consumer price is the consumer share; the fall in the price producers keep is the producer share.","The side of the market that is less price elastic bears the larger share → if demand is more elastic than supply, the consumer price rises by less than half the tax."],"agents":[{"who":"Consumers","what":"Pay a higher price and buy less; those who switch to substitutes avoid part of the burden."},{"who":"Producers","what":"Receive a lower price net of tax, sell less and may cut output or employment."},{"who":"Government","what":"Collects tax revenue equal to the tax per unit multiplied by the new quantity."},{"who":"Workers in the industry","what":"May face lower hours or job losses if output falls significantly."}],"diagram":"Draw demand and supply with equilibrium at P₁ and Q₁. Shift supply vertically up by the tax to S + tax. The new consumer price is Pc and the quantity falls to Q₂. Read down to the original supply curve to find Pp, the price producers keep. Shade consumer incidence (Pc − P₁) and producer incidence (P₁ − Pp).","variables":[{"v":"Price paid by consumers","dir":"up","why":"Rises, but by less than the tax because some buyers reduce purchases as the price rises."},{"v":"Price received by producers","dir":"down","why":"Producers absorb part of the tax to avoid losing too many sales."},{"v":"Quantity traded","dir":"down","why":"The higher consumer price and lower producer price both reduce the equilibrium quantity."},{"v":"Government revenue","dir":"up","why":"Equals the tax per unit multiplied by the new, lower quantity sold."},{"v":"Consumer and producer surplus","dir":"down","why":"Both groups lose surplus; part of the loss becomes tax revenue and part is a welfare loss."}],"short":"In the short run, supply is often less elastic, because firms cannot quickly move resources elsewhere. Producers may then bear a larger share of the tax, so the consumer price rises by less. Consumption falls by more when demand is elastic. The government gains revenue, and if the good has negative externalities, the fall in quantity may reduce external costs.","long":"In the longer run, supply tends to become more elastic as firms leave the industry or shift to other products. More of the tax may then be passed on to consumers through higher prices. Demand may also become more elastic as consumers find substitutes. The split of the burden can therefore change over time, even if the tax rate stays the same.","evidence":"Supporting evidence would be a consumer price rise smaller than the tax, with producer margins falling. Studies of excise taxes generally find that pass-through to consumers varies with market structure and elasticities; it can be close to full in some markets and partial in others, and in imperfectly competitive markets it can sometimes exceed the tax. Data on prices before and after the tax date, compared with untaxed areas, can help isolate the effect.","cases":[],"evaluation":["It depends on the relative price elasticities of demand and supply: the less elastic side bears more of the tax.","In the short run, producers may absorb more of the tax if they cannot easily reduce output.","In the long run, supply tends to become more elastic, so more of the tax may be passed on to consumers.","A key limitation is that the model assumes perfect competition; firms with market power may pass on more or less than the competitive model predicts.","Stakeholders may experience the tax differently: low-income consumers of necessities may lose a larger share of income than richer consumers."],"alternatives":[{"h":"Falling costs or demand at the same time","p":"If input costs fell or demand weakened around the date of the tax, the price may have risen by less for reasons unrelated to incidence. The observed price change would then mix the effect of the tax with the effect of these other shifts in demand or supply.","test":"Compare the taxed market with a similar untaxed market over the same period, and check input-cost data."},{"h":"Firms absorbing the tax for strategic reasons","p":"Firms with market power may delay passing on the tax to protect market share, or may reformulate products to lower the tax due, rather than because supply is inelastic. Such behaviour falls outside the simple competitive model and may change once the tax has been in place for a while.","test":"Check whether firms changed product size or content after the tax and whether margins recovered later."}],"hl":null,"next":["subsidy","externality"]},{"id":"subsidy","q":"Why did output rise after a subsidy, and who gained?","unit":2,"subs":["2.7","2.6","2.5"],"kc":["Intervention","Equity","Efficiency"],"dg":"subsidy","dg2":null,"event":"A government pays producers a fixed amount for each unit of a product they sell. After the subsidy, the quantity produced and sold rises, and the market price paid by consumers falls. Producers receive more per unit than before, once the subsidy is included. The government’s spending rises.","initial":"The market is assumed to be competitive. The size of the effect depends on the price elasticities of demand and supply: if both are fairly elastic, quantity rises a lot. The government must be able to fund the subsidy, and the subsidy is assumed to be paid per unit produced, not as a lump sum.","mechanism":["The subsidy lowers firms’ effective cost per unit → the supply curve shifts vertically downwards by the amount of the subsidy (S to S − subsidy).","At the old price there is now excess supply → the price falls and the market moves along the demand curve to a higher quantity.","Consumers buy more as the price falls → quantity demanded rises, a movement along the demand curve, not a shift of demand.","Producers receive the lower market price plus the subsidy → the price they receive is higher than before, so they expand output.","The benefit is shared: the less price elastic side of the market gains a larger share of the subsidy per unit."],"agents":[{"who":"Producers","what":"Receive a higher price per unit including the subsidy and expand output."},{"who":"Consumers","what":"Pay a lower price and buy more."},{"who":"Government","what":"Pays the subsidy per unit multiplied by the new, higher quantity, which has an opportunity cost."},{"who":"Taxpayers","what":"Fund the subsidy through taxes or borrowing, now or in the future."},{"who":"Foreign producers","what":"May lose market share if the subsidised domestic product competes with imports or exports."}],"diagram":"Draw demand and supply with equilibrium at P₁ and Q₁. Shift supply vertically down by the subsidy to S − subsidy. The new market price paid by consumers falls to Pc, quantity rises to Q₂, and the price producers receive (Pc plus the subsidy) is Pp. Shade the total cost to government: the subsidy per unit times Q₂.","variables":[{"v":"Quantity produced and sold","dir":"up","why":"Lower costs per unit lead producers to supply more at each price."},{"v":"Price paid by consumers","dir":"down","why":"The rightward shift of supply lowers the equilibrium market price."},{"v":"Price received by producers","dir":"up","why":"Producers receive the new market price plus the subsidy per unit."},{"v":"Government spending","dir":"up","why":"Equals the subsidy per unit multiplied by the new, higher quantity."},{"v":"Allocative efficiency","dir":"ambiguous","why":"Falls if the market was efficient before; may rise if the good has positive externalities."}],"short":"In the short run, consumers pay less and buy more, and producers expand output and may hire workers. If supply is inelastic in the short run, producers may capture much of the subsidy as a higher price received, with a smaller rise in output. The government’s budget bears the full cost, which has an opportunity cost in other spending.","long":"In the longer run, supply usually becomes more elastic, so output may rise further. Firms may grow to depend on the subsidy and resist its removal. If the subsidy supports a good with positive externalities, or a new industry that can later compete without help, it may raise welfare. Otherwise it can lock resources into inefficient uses.","evidence":"Supporting evidence would include a rise in output and a fall in consumer prices after the subsidy began, compared with similar unsubsidised markets. Studies generally find that the split of benefits depends on elasticities and market structure, and that subsidies are hard to withdraw once established. Industrial subsidies are sometimes credited with building new industries, but other factors such as scale, competition and technology usually contribute as well.","cases":["MIC-008"],"evaluation":["It depends on the price elasticities of demand and supply, which decide how much output rises and how the gain is shared.","In the short run, inelastic supply means more of the subsidy goes to producers as a higher price received.","In the long run, subsidies can become hard to remove, as firms and workers come to rely on them.","A key limitation is the opportunity cost: the funds could have been spent on other priorities or left with taxpayers.","There may be unintended consequences, such as overproduction, environmental harm or trade disputes with countries whose producers lose market share."],"alternatives":[{"h":"Rising demand at the same time","p":"If demand was rising, for example because incomes grew or tastes changed, output would have risen anyway. In that case, the subsidy explains only part of the increase, and attributing all of the extra output to the policy would overstate its effect.","test":"Check whether prices rose or fell: a supply-side subsidy lowers the market price, while a demand increase raises it."},{"h":"Technological change lowering costs","p":"Falling production costs from new technology also shift supply right, lowering prices and raising output, independently of the subsidy. Where costs fall quickly, as with some new technologies, separating the two effects is difficult, and the subsidy may receive credit for gains that would have happened anyway.","test":"Compare cost trends in subsidised and unsubsidised countries or periods for the same product."}],"hl":null,"next":["tax","tariff"]},{"id":"tariff","q":"Why did domestic producers gain but consumers lose after a tariff?","unit":4,"subs":["4.2","4.3","4.1"],"kc":["Interdependence","Intervention","Equity"],"dg":"tariff","dg2":null,"event":"A country imposes a tariff on an imported good that it also produces at home. After the tariff, the domestic price of the good rises. Domestic producers sell more and earn more, imports fall, and consumers pay more and buy less. The government receives tariff revenue on the imports that remain.","initial":"The country is assumed to be a small economy that takes the world price as given, with domestic supply that cannot meet all demand at the world price, so imports fill the gap. The effects depend on the elasticities of domestic demand and supply and on whether other countries retaliate.","mechanism":["Before the tariff, the domestic price equals the world price → domestic producers supply part of demand and imports fill the rest.","The tariff raises the price of imports to the world price plus the tariff → the domestic price rises to this level.","Domestic producers respond to the higher price → they move up their supply curve and increase output, gaining producer surplus.","Consumers respond to the higher price → they move up the demand curve and buy less, losing consumer surplus.","With domestic output up and consumption down, imports fall → the government collects revenue on remaining imports, and some resources are used less efficiently."],"agents":[{"who":"Domestic producers","what":"Sell more at a higher price and gain producer surplus; may hire more workers."},{"who":"Consumers","what":"Pay a higher price, buy less and lose consumer surplus."},{"who":"Government","what":"Collects tariff revenue equal to the tariff per unit times the quantity still imported."},{"who":"Foreign producers","what":"Sell less in the protected market and may lobby their governments to retaliate."},{"who":"Downstream firms","what":"Pay more if the good is an input, which raises their costs and may cut their output."}],"diagram":"Draw domestic demand and supply with the world price Pw below the domestic equilibrium. Show imports as the gap between domestic quantity demanded and supplied at Pw. Draw Pw + tariff; domestic output rises, consumption falls and imports shrink. Label the gain in producer surplus, tariff revenue and the two welfare-loss triangles.","variables":[{"v":"Domestic price","dir":"up","why":"The tariff raises the price of imports, and domestic producers can match it."},{"v":"Domestic production","dir":"up","why":"Producers move up their supply curve in response to the higher price."},{"v":"Domestic consumption","dir":"down","why":"Consumers move up the demand curve and buy less at the higher price."},{"v":"Imports","dir":"down","why":"The gap between domestic demand and domestic supply narrows from both sides."},{"v":"Consumer surplus","dir":"down","why":"Consumers pay more for fewer units; their loss exceeds the gains to producers and government."}],"short":"In the short run, protected producers gain sales, profits and possibly jobs, while consumers pay more. The government gains revenue. Firms that use the good as an input face higher costs, which can reduce their competitiveness and employment. The total loss to consumers is larger than the combined gain to producers and government, leaving a net welfare loss.","long":"In the longer run, protected firms may face less pressure to cut costs or innovate, so inefficiency can persist. Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs on the country’s exports, harming exporters. If the tariff protects a genuine infant industry that later becomes competitive, the long-run gains might offset the costs, but this outcome is uncertain and hard to judge in advance.","evidence":"Supporting evidence would include a rise in domestic prices close to the tariff, higher domestic output and lower imports in the protected industry. Studies of recent tariffs generally find that much of the cost was passed on to domestic buyers, and that downstream industries using the protected good were often harmed. The overall employment effect is usually found to be small or negative once retaliation and input costs are included.","cases":["GLO-002","GLO-043"],"evaluation":["It depends on the elasticities of domestic demand and supply, which decide how far output, consumption and imports change.","In the short run, protected producers and their workers gain visibly, while the cost to consumers is spread thinly and may go unnoticed.","In the long run, protection may reduce the pressure to become efficient, unless the tariff is temporary and tied to clear targets.","This may be weaker if trading partners retaliate, because exporters then lose markets.","Stakeholders may experience the tariff very differently: low-income consumers may lose a larger share of income, while protected firms gain."],"alternatives":[{"h":"A rise in the world price","p":"If the world price rose at the same time, for example because of a global supply shortage, domestic prices and output would rise even without the tariff. Domestic producers would gain and consumers lose, but the cause would lie in the world market rather than in trade protection.","test":"Compare the domestic price with the world price before and after; with a tariff, the gap between them widens by about the tariff."},{"h":"A depreciation of the currency","p":"A weaker currency raises the domestic price of all imports and helps domestic producers, producing similar effects to a tariff across many goods. Unlike a tariff, it also makes exports cheaper abroad and raises no revenue for the government.","test":"Check whether the price rise was limited to the tariffed good or spread across many imported goods."}],"hl":"HL: calculating the welfare effects of the tariff (changes in consumer and producer surplus, tariff revenue and welfare loss)","next":["subsidy","currency"]},{"id":"supplyside","q":"Why might a supply-side reform take years to show results?","unit":3,"subs":["3.7","3.2","4.10"],"kc":["Change","Efficiency","Economic well-being"],"dg":"growth","dg2":"lras","event":"A government introduces a supply-side reform, such as investment in education and training, infrastructure, competition policy or changes to labour-market rules. Several years later, commentators note that growth and productivity have improved only modestly so far, and debate whether the reform is working or has failed.","initial":"The reform aims to raise the quantity or quality of factors of production, or the efficiency with which they are used. Results depend on how large the reform is, whether it is credible and sustained, the starting levels of skills and infrastructure, and whether demand is strong enough for firms to use any new capacity.","mechanism":["The reform aims to raise productive capacity → if successful, it shifts LRAS right, but only once the underlying factors have changed.","Education and training take years before students enter the workforce → the stock of human capital grows slowly.","Infrastructure must be planned, built and then used → firms change where and how they produce only after projects are complete.","Market-based reforms change incentives → but firms and workers adjust gradually, and some adjustment, such as job losses in declining firms, may come first.","Once the factors have improved, productivity rises → LRAS shifts right, potential output grows and the economy can grow faster without extra inflationary pressure."],"agents":[{"who":"Government","what":"Designs, funds and must sustain the reform across several budgets and possibly elections."},{"who":"Workers and students","what":"Acquire new skills or adjust to new rules, which takes time and effort."},{"who":"Firms","what":"Decide whether to invest, enter new markets or change production methods as conditions change."},{"who":"Investors","what":"Wait to see if the reform is credible and lasting before committing capital."}],"diagram":"Draw the long-run growth diagram with LRAS₁ at potential output Yp₁. Show LRAS shifting right to LRAS₂, increasing potential output to Yp₂, and note that the shift happens gradually, over years. Optionally show AD shifting right too, so actual output rises with potential and the price level stays broadly stable.","variables":[{"v":"Potential output","dir":"up","why":"Rises slowly as skills, infrastructure and efficiency improve; little changes in the first years."},{"v":"Labour productivity","dir":"up","why":"Better skills, capital and organisation raise output per worker, usually after a lag."},{"v":"Government spending","dir":"up","why":"Interventionist reforms such as education and infrastructure need funding before benefits appear."},{"v":"Structural unemployment","dir":"ambiguous","why":"May rise at first as workers leave declining firms, then fall as skills improve."},{"v":"Inflationary pressure","dir":"down","why":"Over time, more capacity lets output grow faster without pushing up the price level."}],"short":"In the short run, the reform mainly creates costs and spending. Interventionist policies raise government spending, which may add to AD before it adds to capacity. Market-based reforms can cause job losses or resistance as firms and workers adjust. Measured growth may change little, because capacity has not yet expanded. This can make a reform look like a failure.","long":"In the longer run, if the reform is sustained, better skills, infrastructure and competition can raise productivity and shift LRAS right. Potential growth rises, and the economy can grow faster with less inflationary pressure. The benefits may be uneven across regions and groups. If the reform is reversed or poorly designed, the gains may not appear at all.","evidence":"Supporting evidence would include gradual improvements in skills, infrastructure quality or business investment, followed later by rising productivity. Studies generally find that education and infrastructure have positive long-run effects on growth, but the size and timing vary widely. Isolating a reform’s effect is difficult because many other factors change over the same years, so evidence is often based on comparisons across regions or countries.","cases":["MAC-033","DEV-036"],"evaluation":["It depends on the type of reform: education and infrastructure may take a decade, while some deregulation can work faster.","In the short run, the costs are visible and the benefits are not, which can make reforms politically difficult to sustain.","In the long run, successful reforms can raise potential growth without adding to inflation.","A key limitation is that it is hard to separate a reform’s effects from other changes over the same period.","Stakeholders may experience the reform unevenly: workers in declining sectors may lose out before the wider benefits appear."],"alternatives":[{"h":"The reform was poorly designed or not implemented","p":"Slow results may reflect weak implementation, underfunding or reversal, rather than normal time lags. The reform may not deliver the expected effect at all. In that case, waiting longer will not help, and the design or funding of the policy needs to be reviewed.","test":"Check whether intermediate indicators, such as school enrolment or project completion, improved as planned."},{"h":"Weak aggregate demand","p":"If demand is weak, firms may not use the new capacity, so output does not rise even though LRAS has shifted. The problem would then be on the demand side, and demand-side policy may be needed alongside the reform.","test":"Look for spare capacity, falling inflation and high cyclical unemployment alongside the reform."}],"hl":null,"next":["growth","unemployment"]},{"id":"externality","q":"Why did the market produce too much pollution?","unit":2,"subs":["2.8","2.7","4.7"],"kc":["Sustainability","Efficiency","Intervention"],"dg":"negprod","dg2":"tax","event":"An industry, such as power generation or manufacturing, emits pollution that damages the health of nearby residents and the environment. Output and pollution are higher than many people think is reasonable. Those harmed do not receive compensation, and the price of the product does not reflect the damage caused.","initial":"The market is competitive, and firms make decisions based on their private costs. The pollution harms third parties who are not part of the transaction. There is no effective tax, regulation or tradable permit system in place, and property rights over clean air or water are unclear or hard to enforce.","mechanism":["Firms choose output where price equals their marginal private cost (MPC) → the cost of pollution to third parties is not included in their decisions.","The pollution imposes an external cost → marginal social cost (MSC) lies above MPC by the amount of the external cost per unit.","The market equilibrium is at Qm, where demand (MSB) meets MPC → but the socially optimal output is at Q*, where MSB meets MSC.","Because Qm is greater than Q*, too much is produced → too many resources are allocated to the good and too much pollution is emitted.","The units between Q* and Qm cost society more than they are worth → this creates a welfare loss and market failure."],"agents":[{"who":"Firms","what":"Produce where price equals private marginal cost, ignoring costs they do not pay."},{"who":"Consumers","what":"Buy the good at a price that does not reflect the full social cost, so they buy more."},{"who":"Third parties","what":"Bear health and environmental costs without compensation."},{"who":"Government","what":"May intervene through taxes, regulation or tradable permits to reduce output towards Q*."}],"diagram":"Draw MPC and MSC, with MSC above MPC by the external cost, and demand as MSB. Mark the market output Qm, where MSB = MPC, and the social optimum Q*, where MSB = MSC. Shade the welfare-loss triangle between Q* and Qm, between MSC and MSB. Show that the market overproduces and underprices the good.","variables":[{"v":"Output of the good","dir":"up","why":"The market produces Qm, above the socially optimal Q*, because external costs are ignored."},{"v":"Pollution","dir":"up","why":"Higher output brings more emissions than would occur at the social optimum."},{"v":"Market price","dir":"down","why":"The price reflects only private costs, so it is lower than the price at the social optimum."},{"v":"Social welfare","dir":"down","why":"Units beyond Q* add more to social cost than to social benefit, creating a welfare loss."}],"short":"In the short run, consumers enjoy a lower price and firms earn revenue from higher output, while third parties bear health and environmental costs. These costs may not show up in market prices or GDP, so they can be overlooked. The welfare loss is a measure of how much better off society could be with less output.","long":"In the longer run, pollution can accumulate, damaging health, ecosystems and the productivity of land and water. This can reduce future growth and well-being, raising questions about sustainability. If the government introduces a tax, permits or regulation, firms may invest in cleaner technology, which can reduce the external cost per unit over time.","evidence":"Supporting evidence would include measured damage to health or the environment near polluting sites, and prices that do not change with the level of pollution. Studies generally find that pricing pollution, through taxes or tradable permits, tends to reduce emissions, though the size of the effect varies. Measuring the external cost precisely is difficult, so estimates of the optimal output differ.","cases":["MIC-010","MIC-048"],"evaluation":["It depends on the size of the external cost, which is hard to measure precisely and may vary across places and people.","In the short run, a tax or regulation may raise prices and reduce output and jobs in the polluting industry.","In the long run, pricing pollution can encourage cleaner technology, reducing the external cost per unit.","A key limitation is that governments may set the tax or cap at the wrong level, because they lack full information.","Stakeholders may experience pollution and policy unequally: low-income households may live closer to polluting sites and also pay more of a tax as a share of income."],"alternatives":[{"h":"Government failure rather than market failure","p":"Some pollution may persist because existing regulations are weakly enforced or because subsidies encourage polluting activities, not only because the market ignores external costs. In that case, the government’s own policies are part of the problem, and removing harmful subsidies may help more than a new tax.","test":"Check whether there are rules or subsidies in place, and whether pollution is higher where enforcement is weaker."},{"h":"High demand for the good","p":"Pollution may rise because demand for the product grew, even if the gap between private and social cost did not change. More output then means more pollution, but the externality per unit, and so the market failure, has not grown.","test":"Compare emissions per unit of output over time: if they are stable, the rise reflects higher output, not a larger externality."}],"hl":null,"next":["tax","subsidy"]}],"v2":{"schools":[{"id":"classical","name":"Classical political economy","span":"1776–1870s","summary":"Explained growth, value and distribution through labour, capital, land and competition, generally favouring freer trade over mercantilist restriction."},{"id":"marxian","name":"Marxian economics","span":"1840s onwards","summary":"Reworked classical value theory into a critique of capitalism, focusing on class, exploitation, accumulation and recurring crises."},{"id":"neoclassical","name":"Neoclassical (marginalist) economics","span":"1870s onwards","summary":"Analyses choices at the margin, with prices set by demand and supply; built the partial-equilibrium tools still used in introductory courses."},{"id":"welfare","name":"Welfare economics","span":"1920s onwards","summary":"Asks how to judge whether one economic outcome is better than another, from social cost and externalities to social choice and capabilities."},{"id":"keynesian","name":"Keynesian and post-Keynesian economics","span":"1930s onwards","summary":"Treats aggregate demand as a driver of output and employment, so economies can settle below full employment without policy action."},{"id":"austrian","name":"Austrian school","span":"1870s onwards","summary":"Stresses dispersed knowledge, entrepreneurship and market processes over time; sceptical of central planning and discretionary macroeconomic management."},{"id":"chicago","name":"Monetarism and the Chicago school","span":"1930s onwards","summary":"Emphasised money supply, expectations and rules over discretion, and generally favoured markets over government intervention."},{"id":"institutional","name":"New institutional economics and political economy","span":"1970s onwards","summary":"Explains economic outcomes through rules, property rights, norms and political power, including institutions that communities design for themselves."},{"id":"information","name":"Information economics","span":"1970s onwards","summary":"Studies how asymmetric and imperfect information changes market outcomes, producing adverse selection, moral hazard, signalling and rationing."},{"id":"experimental","name":"Experimental development economics","span":"1990s onwards","summary":"Uses randomised controlled trials and close fieldwork to test which specific policies improve the lives of poor households."}],"economists":{"smith":{"school":"classical","majorWork":{"t":"An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations","y":1776},"question":"What makes a nation wealthy, and why do some economies grow while others stagnate?","core":"Wealth lies in productive labour, not stocks of gold; specialisation and competitive exchange raise output, within a framework of law, institutions and moral norms.","chain":["Division of labour","Rising output per worker","Specialisation across producers","Limited by extent of the market","Case for wider trade"],"chainNote":"The first four steps follow Smith’s argument in Book I; his case for freer trade is made mainly in Book IV. Linking it to later free-trade policy is a reading of his influence, not his prediction.","builtOn":[],"challenged":[],"outside":[],"cambridge":["division of labour","specialisation","price mechanism","free trade","protectionism","role of government"],"university":"History of economic thought and growth theory: specialisation and market size reappear in formal models of increasing returns and trade.","india":"","relevance":"Debates on global supply chains, competition policy and collusion, and the role of trust and norms in exchange still use questions Smith raised."},"ricardo":{"school":"classical","majorWork":{"t":"On the Principles of Political Economy and Taxation","y":1817},"question":"How is national income divided between landlords, capitalists and workers, and how does trade affect it?","core":"Countries gain from trade by specialising according to comparative, not absolute, advantage; rising rents on scarce land can squeeze profits and slow growth.","chain":["Smith’s absolute advantage","Relative opportunity costs","Comparative advantage","Gains from specialisation and trade","The classical case for free trade"],"chainNote":"The chain traces Ricardo’s trade argument; his opposition to the Corn Laws, set out in 1815 before comparative advantage, rested mainly on his theory of rent and falling profits; the wine and cloth example is a simplified two-good illustration.","builtOn":[{"id":"smith","why":"Extended Smith’s absolute-advantage case for trade to comparative advantage."}],"challenged":[{"id":"malthus","why":"Disagreed with Malthus over the Corn Laws and whether a general glut of goods was possible."}],"outside":[],"cambridge":["comparative advantage","absolute advantage","opportunity cost","free trade","protectionism","terms of trade"],"university":"International trade theory: Ricardian models with many goods, technology differences and productivity, extended later by factor-endowment and new trade models.","india":"","relevance":"Comparative advantage underpins debates about outsourcing, import competition, reshoring and industrial policy, many of which test the limits of his assumptions."},"malthus":{"school":"classical","majorWork":{"t":"An Essay on the Principle of Population","y":1798},"question":"Can living standards rise for long if population tends to grow faster than food supply?","core":"Population tends to outrun food production, so without checks on births, gains in living standards are likely to be eroded.","chain":["Population grows geometrically","Food supply grows arithmetically","Pressure on subsistence","Preventive and positive checks","Pessimism about lasting improvement"],"chainNote":"This is Malthus’s own reasoning. Later growth in farm productivity and falling birth rates are the main reasons historians treat it as conditional.","builtOn":[],"challenged":[{"id":"ricardo","why":"Argued against Ricardo that a general glut of goods, from insufficient demand, was possible."}],"outside":[{"name":"William Godwin","rel":"challenged","why":"The Essay was written partly in reply to Godwin’s optimism about the improvement of society."}],"cambridge":["scarcity","population growth","diminishing returns","economic growth","sustainability"],"university":"Economic history and growth theory: the ‘Malthusian trap’ appears in unified growth models explaining the long pre-industrial stagnation of living standards.","india":"","relevance":"Arguments about whether natural resources and environmental limits constrain long-run growth echo Malthus; critics stress prices, substitution and innovation."},"marx":{"school":"marxian","majorWork":{"t":"Capital, Volume I","y":1867},"question":"Where do profits come from in a capitalist economy, and why is it prone to crisis?","core":"Profit arises from surplus value produced by labour; competition drives accumulation and mechanisation, producing inequality, concentration and recurring crises.","chain":["Classical labour theory of value","Labour power as a commodity","Surplus value and profit","Accumulation and mechanisation","Concentration and recurring crises"],"chainNote":"This sets out Marx’s argument in Capital. Whether its predictions held, and how far planned economies reflected it, are separate and contested questions.","builtOn":[{"id":"ricardo","why":"Took Ricardo’s labour theory of value and class-based account of distribution as his starting point."},{"id":"smith","why":"Read Smith closely and reworked the classical labour theory of value."}],"challenged":[{"id":"ricardo","why":"Argued classical economists treated capitalism’s categories as natural rather than historically specific."}],"outside":[{"name":"G. W. F. Hegel","rel":"built on","why":"Adapted Hegel’s dialectical method into a materialist account of history."}],"cambridge":["economic systems","planned economy","free market economy","income distribution","inequality","labour market"],"university":"Political economy and history of thought: formal debates on the transformation problem, the labour share and theories of crisis and accumulation.","india":"","relevance":"Debates about automation and jobs, the falling labour share and concentrated market power echo questions Marx raised, though rarely his policy conclusions."},"marshall":{"school":"neoclassical","majorWork":{"t":"Principles of Economics","y":1890},"question":"How do demand and supply together determine price and quantity in a single market?","core":"Price is set by demand and supply acting together, like scissor blades; marginal tools such as elasticity and consumer surplus analyse each market separately.","chain":["Marginal utility and cost","Demand and supply schedules","Partial equilibrium in one market","Elasticity and consumer surplus","Time periods: short and long run"],"chainNote":"The chain shows how Marshall assembled the toolkit students use; holding other markets constant is a simplifying choice, not a description of reality.","builtOn":[{"id":"ricardo","why":"Treated his work as continuous with classical economics, joining cost of production to marginal utility."}],"challenged":[],"outside":[],"cambridge":["demand","supply","price elasticity of demand","consumer surplus","producer surplus","market equilibrium","economies of scale"],"university":"Microeconomics: partial equilibrium is set against general equilibrium, and consumer surplus is derived from utility and demand functions.","india":"","relevance":"Supply and demand diagrams, elasticity and surplus remain everyday tools for regulators and tax analysts; external economies help explain industrial clusters."},"pigou":{"school":"welfare","majorWork":{"t":"The Economics of Welfare","y":1920},"question":"When do private decisions fail to maximise social welfare, and how could government correct them?","core":"Where private and social costs or benefits diverge, markets misallocate resources; a tax or subsidy equal to the gap can improve welfare.","chain":["Marshall’s external economies","Private versus social cost","Externalities cause misallocation","Corrective tax or subsidy","Later carbon and congestion pricing"],"chainNote":"The last step is later policy uptake of Pigou’s idea, shaped by many others, rather than something Pigou designed.","builtOn":[{"id":"marshall","why":"Built welfare economics on Marshall’s consumer surplus and his idea of external effects."}],"challenged":[],"outside":[{"name":"Ronald Coase","rel":"influenced","why":"Coase’s 1960 paper criticised the Pigouvian tax approach, stressing bargaining and transaction costs."}],"cambridge":["externalities","market failure","social cost","indirect taxes","subsidies","demerit goods"],"university":"Public economics: optimal corrective taxation, comparison with tradable permits, and the Coase theorem on bargaining and property rights.","india":"","relevance":"Carbon pricing, congestion charges and taxes on demerit goods apply Pigou’s reasoning; debate centres on information, feasibility and distributional effects."},"keynes":{"school":"keynesian","majorWork":{"t":"The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money","y":1936},"question":"Why can an economy remain stuck with mass unemployment, and what can policy do about it?","core":"Output and employment depend on aggregate demand; when spending falls, an economy can settle below full employment, creating a case for policy intervention.","chain":["Great Depression","Falling aggregate demand","Underemployment equilibrium","Case for fiscal intervention","Postwar demand management"],"chainNote":"The Depression framed Keynes’s argument; the final step is how governments later used it, which went beyond and sometimes differed from his own proposals.","builtOn":[{"id":"marshall","why":"Trained in Marshall’s Cambridge economics, whose methods he used before breaking with it on aggregate demand."},{"id":"malthus","why":"Saw Malthus’s concern with general gluts as a forerunner of effective demand."}],"challenged":[{"id":"pigou","why":"Cast Pigou’s theory of unemployment as the classical view the General Theory set out to replace."}],"outside":[{"name":"Richard Kahn","rel":"built on","why":"Used Kahn’s 1931 employment multiplier in developing the General Theory."}],"cambridge":["aggregate demand","unemployment","fiscal policy","multiplier","government spending","recession"],"university":"Macroeconomics: IS–LM and New Keynesian models formalise demand, sticky prices and expectations; debates focus on fiscal multipliers.","india":"Keynes’s first book, Indian Currency and Finance (1913), analysed India’s monetary system; he had earlier worked at the India Office in London.","relevance":"Debates about stimulus, austerity, fiscal multipliers and public debt after 2008 and during COVID-19 draw on Keynesian reasoning."},"robinson":{"school":"keynesian","majorWork":{"t":"The Economics of Imperfect Competition","y":1933},"question":"How do prices, output and wages behave when firms can set prices rather than take them?","core":"Most markets lie between perfect competition and monopoly; firms with market power restrict output, and employers with monopsony power can pay below marginal revenue product.","chain":["Marshall’s competitive firm","Downward-sloping firm demand curves","Marginal revenue and price-setting","Monopsony in labour markets","Wider critique of equilibrium economics"],"chainNote":"The early steps are her 1933 book; the last summarises her later post-Keynesian work, which developed over several decades.","builtOn":[{"id":"marshall","why":"Rebuilt Marshall’s theory of the firm for markets with price-setting firms."},{"id":"keynes","why":"Part of the Cambridge group that helped develop and extend Keynes’s theory."}],"challenged":[],"outside":[{"name":"Paul Samuelson","rel":"challenged","why":"Opposed Samuelson and other MIT economists in the Cambridge capital controversy."}],"cambridge":["imperfect competition","monopoly","monopsony","price discrimination","market power","labour market"],"university":"Industrial organisation and labour economics: formal models of oligopoly, price discrimination and monopsony wage-setting.","india":"","relevance":"Debates on large digital platforms, supermarkets’ power over suppliers and minimum wages in monopsonistic labour markets use concepts she developed."},"hayek":{"school":"austrian","majorWork":{"t":"The Use of Knowledge in Society","y":1945},"question":"How can an economy use knowledge that is dispersed among millions of people and held by no single mind?","core":"Prices transmit dispersed, local knowledge that no planner could gather, allowing coordination without central direction; this underpins his critique of planning.","chain":["Knowledge dispersed among individuals","Prices summarise local information","Decentralised coordination","Limits of central planning","Case for market order"],"chainNote":"The chain is Hayek’s own argument; many critics accept the knowledge problem but question how far it rules out regulation or public provision.","builtOn":[{"id":"smith","why":"Developed Smith’s idea of an unplanned market order into an argument about knowledge and prices."}],"challenged":[{"id":"keynes","why":"Debated Keynes in the 1930s over the causes of slumps and the role of policy."},{"id":"marx","why":"His knowledge argument became a central objection to economies planned in Marx’s name."}],"outside":[{"name":"Ludwig von Mises","rel":"built on","why":"Developed Mises’s critique of economic calculation under socialism."}],"cambridge":["price mechanism","planned economy","free market economy","resource allocation","government failure"],"university":"Comparative economic systems and mechanism design: the socialist calculation debate and formal models of information in prices.","india":"","relevance":"Relevant to debates on whether data and algorithms could support central coordination, to price-based policy design, and to credit-fuelled asset booms."},"friedman":{"school":"chicago","majorWork":{"t":"A Monetary History of the United States, 1867–1960 (with Anna Schwartz)","y":1963},"question":"How much do changes in the money supply explain inflation and the depth of recessions?","core":"Sustained inflation is mainly a monetary phenomenon; discretionary demand management is unreliable, and no lasting trade-off exists between inflation and unemployment.","chain":["Quantity theory of money","Monetary contraction deepened the Depression","Adaptive expectations","Natural rate of unemployment","Rules over discretion"],"chainNote":"These are the main steps in Friedman’s case; the reading of the Depression is his and Schwartz’s, and remains debated alongside other explanations.","builtOn":[],"challenged":[{"id":"keynes","why":"His monetarism was the leading challenge to postwar Keynesian demand management."}],"outside":[],"cambridge":["monetary policy","money supply","inflation","quantity theory of money","Phillips curve","expectations"],"university":"Monetary economics: expectations-augmented Phillips curve, the natural rate hypothesis and the rules-versus-discretion debate in central banking.","india":"","relevance":"Central banks now target inflation rather than money growth, but his stress on expectations shapes their communication; 2008 responses drew on his reading of the Depression."},"sen":{"school":"welfare","majorWork":{"t":"Development as Freedom","y":1999},"question":"What should development mean, and why do famines occur even when food is available?","core":"Development is the expansion of people’s real freedoms and capabilities, not income alone; famines reflect collapsing entitlements as much as falling food supply.","chain":["Limits of income as welfare","Entitlements and famine","Capabilities and functionings","Development as expanding freedoms","Human Development Index"],"chainNote":"The steps follow Sen’s work from the late 1970s to 1999; the HDI (1990) predates Development as Freedom and is a simplified measure inspired by his approach, not a full statement of it.","builtOn":[{"id":"smith","why":"Draws on Smith’s moral philosophy and his account of what people need to live without shame."}],"challenged":[{"id":"malthus","why":"His entitlement analysis challenged explanations of famine based only on food supply."}],"outside":[{"name":"Kenneth Arrow","rel":"built on","why":"Extended Arrow’s social choice theory, which Sen developed in his early work."},{"name":"Mahbub ul Haq","rel":"influenced","why":"The capability approach informed the Human Development Index that Haq led at the UNDP."}],"cambridge":["economic development","Human Development Index","poverty","inequality","living standards","GDP per capita"],"university":"Development and welfare economics: capability theory, social choice, multidimensional poverty indices and entitlement-based analysis of food crises.","india":"Poverty and Famines analyses the 1943 Bengal famine; his work shapes debates on Indian hunger, education, health and the HDI.","relevance":"Informs multidimensional poverty measures, debates about the limits of GDP per capita, and responses to hunger during food price shocks."},"ostrom":{"school":"institutional","majorWork":{"t":"Governing the Commons: The Evolution of Institutions for Collective Action","y":1990},"question":"Can communities manage shared resources sustainably without either state control or privatisation?","core":"Users of common pool resources can sustain them through self-designed rules, monitoring and sanctions when certain institutional conditions, her design principles, are met.","chain":["Tragedy of the commons argument","Field evidence of successful commons","Self-governing rules and monitoring","Design principles for durable institutions","Polycentric governance"],"chainNote":"The chain follows her empirical programme; her design principles describe conditions associated with success, not a formula that suits every resource.","builtOn":[],"challenged":[],"outside":[{"name":"Garrett Hardin","rel":"challenged","why":"Her evidence challenged Hardin’s view that commons lead to ruin without state control or privatisation."}],"cambridge":["common resources","market failure","sustainability","property rights","government intervention"],"university":"Institutional and environmental economics: game theory of collective action, repeated interaction and laboratory and field experiments on cooperation.","india":"Researchers apply her design principles to Indian groundwater, village irrigation and community forest management.","relevance":"Debates on overfishing, groundwater depletion, community forestry and climate cooperation draw on her findings about self-governance."},"stiglitz":{"school":"information","majorWork":{"t":"Globalization and Its Discontents","y":2002},"question":"What happens to market outcomes when buyers, sellers, lenders and borrowers hold different information?","core":"With imperfect and asymmetric information, markets can be inefficient, for example through credit rationing, so well-designed government action may improve outcomes.","chain":["Perfect-information market models","Asymmetric information","Screening and credit rationing","Markets can be inefficient","Critique of IMF crisis policies"],"chainNote":"The early steps are his academic work; the last is his later application of it, set out in Globalization and Its Discontents.","builtOn":[],"challenged":[{"id":"smith","why":"Questioned formal invisible-hand results that rely on perfect information, rather than Smith’s own broader argument."},{"id":"friedman","why":"Challenged the view, associated with Chicago economics, that competitive markets are generally efficient."}],"outside":[],"cambridge":["asymmetric information","market failure","moral hazard","adverse selection","globalisation","government intervention"],"university":"Information economics: screening and signalling models, the Stiglitz–Weiss credit rationing model and efficiency-wage theory.","india":"","relevance":"Insurance, credit scoring, online reviews and data privacy all raise information problems; disclosure, licensing and standards respond to them."},"banerjee":{"school":"experimental","majorWork":{"t":"Poor Economics (with Esther Duflo)","y":2011},"question":"Which specific policies actually improve the lives of poor households, and how can we know?","core":"Development questions can be broken into specific, testable problems; randomised trials and fieldwork reveal how poor households decide and which programmes work.","chain":["Broad debates over aid","Specific, testable policy questions","Randomised controlled trials","Evidence on household decisions","Programme design and scale-up"],"chainNote":"The chain describes the method he and Duflo promoted; critics question how far results from one setting transfer to others.","builtOn":[],"challenged":[],"outside":[],"cambridge":["economic development","poverty","microfinance","aid","education","health"],"university":"Development economics and econometrics: causal inference, randomised evaluation, external validity and behavioural models of poor households.","india":"Born in Mumbai and raised in Kolkata, Banerjee has run many field studies in India, including education programmes with the NGO Pratham.","relevance":"Shapes how governments, charities and aid agencies design and evaluate cash transfers, school programmes, health campaigns and microfinance."},"duflo":{"school":"experimental","majorWork":{"t":"Poor Economics (with Abhijit Banerjee)","y":2011},"question":"How can rigorous evidence identify development policies that help poor households?","core":"Randomised evaluations can isolate the effects of specific policies; small design details often decide whether education, health or credit programmes succeed.","chain":["Specific policy questions","Random assignment of programmes","Measured causal effects","Attention to design details","Evidence-based policy and scale-up"],"chainNote":"This sets out the experimental method she helped spread; how far trial results generalise across contexts remains debated.","builtOn":[],"challenged":[],"outside":[],"cambridge":["economic development","poverty","education","health","gender inequality","aid"],"university":"Development economics and econometrics: randomised evaluation design, causal inference and external validity of field experiments.","india":"Much of her research uses trials in India, including teacher attendance monitoring with the NGO Seva Mandir in Rajasthan.","relevance":"Cash transfers, women’s empowerment, education and health policy debates regularly cite experimental findings her work helped produce."},"acemoglu":{"school":"institutional","majorWork":{"t":"Why Nations Fail (with James Robinson)","y":2012},"question":"Why are some countries rich and others poor, and why do differences persist?","core":"Inclusive economic and political institutions encourage investment and innovation; extractive institutions concentrate power and income, limiting long-run growth.","chain":["Differences in national prosperity","Institutions shape incentives","Inclusive versus extractive institutions","Political power sustains institutions","Reform faces elite resistance"],"chainNote":"The chain summarises the book’s argument; critics debate how institutions are measured and how far geography, culture or policy also matter.","builtOn":[],"challenged":[],"outside":[{"name":"Douglass North","rel":"built on","why":"Built on North’s account of institutions as the rules shaping economic incentives."}],"cambridge":["economic growth","economic development","property rights","corruption","role of government","inequality"],"university":"Political economy and growth: formal models of institutional change and historical natural experiments used to estimate institutional effects.","india":"","relevance":"Shapes debates on weak institutions, corruption and the state in development; his recent work addresses technology, inequality and the labour share."}},"compare":[{"id":"keynes-hayek","a":"keynes","b":"hayek","title":"Keynes and Hayek","frame":"A 1930s exchange over why slumps happen and whether governments should respond by managing demand or allow markets to adjust.","rows":{"question":["Why can an economy stay at high unemployment, and how can policy restore employment?","Why do booms and busts occur, and what are the limits of policy in steering a complex economy?"],"assumptions":["Expectations and uncertainty can keep investment and spending low for long periods; even falling money wages need not restore full employment.","Knowledge is dispersed; market prices coordinate plans, and credit expansion can distort the structure of production."],"mechanism":["Falling aggregate demand reduces output and employment, and the multiplier amplifies the fall.","Credit-driven booms misdirect investment; the bust is the economy correcting earlier distortions."],"policy":["Government spending and lower interest rates to support demand during a slump.","Caution about stimulus; emphasis on stable money, rules and market adjustment."],"criticisms":["Critics question the size of fiscal multipliers, the effect on public debt and how well governments time intervention.","Critics argue that allowing a slump to run its course imposes heavy and avoidable costs in unemployment."],"context":["The 1931–32 exchange with Hayek concerned Keynes’s Treatise on Money (1930); the General Theory (1936), written amid mass unemployment, came later and Hayek never reviewed it in full.","Shaped by Austrian monetary theory; Hayek was at the London School of Economics during the 1930s debate."],"relevance":["Frames debates on stimulus and austerity after 2008 and during the COVID-19 response.","Cited in discussions of asset bubbles and misallocated investment after long periods of low interest rates."]}},{"id":"friedman-keynes","a":"friedman","b":"keynes","title":"Friedman and Keynes","frame":"A postwar debate between Friedman and Keynesian economists, after Keynes’s death in 1946, over what drives inflation and recessions, and whether discretionary demand management stabilises or destabilises the economy.","rows":{"question":["How far do changes in the money supply explain inflation and the depth of recessions?","Why can an economy remain stuck with high unemployment, and what can policy do?"],"assumptions":["Markets tend towards a natural rate of unemployment; people adjust their inflation expectations to experience.","Uncertainty and volatile expectations can leave demand too low for full employment, even if wages fall."],"mechanism":["Excess money growth raises inflation; attempts to hold unemployment below its natural rate only raise inflation over time.","Changes in spending alter output and employment through the multiplier, with limited automatic correction."],"policy":["Stable, rule-based monetary growth; scepticism about fine-tuning with either monetary or fiscal policy, given long and variable lags.","Active fiscal policy and low interest rates to support demand in a slump."],"criticisms":["Critics note that money supply targets were hard to use in practice as money demand became unstable.","Critics point to 1970s inflation under postwar Keynesian policies, which went beyond Keynes’s own proposals, and to doubts about how accurately governments can time intervention."],"context":["Developed in the 1950s and 1960s and influential after stagflation in the 1970s.","Developed in response to the Great Depression and mass unemployment of the 1930s."],"relevance":["Central banks’ attention to inflation expectations and credibility reflects his influence.","Fiscal stimulus debates after 2008 and during COVID-19 draw on his reasoning."]}},{"id":"smith-marx","a":"smith","b":"marx","title":"Smith and Marx","frame":"Two readings of a market economy: one stressing specialisation and growth through exchange, the other stressing class relations, exploitation and crisis.","rows":{"question":["What makes a nation wealthy, and how do markets coordinate production without central direction?","Where do profits come from under capitalism, and why is it prone to inequality and crisis?"],"assumptions":["Self-interest operates within law, competition and moral norms; productive labour is the source of wealth.","Labour creates value; the relationship between owners of capital and wage workers shapes distribution."],"mechanism":["Division of labour raises productivity, and competition guides resources through prices towards what people want.","Capitalists gain surplus value from labour; competition drives accumulation, mechanisation and concentration."],"policy":["Removing monopoly privileges and trade restrictions, with the state providing defence, justice and public works.","Replacing capitalist ownership of the means of production, an argument made more in his political writing."],"criticisms":["Critics argue he underrated market power, inequality and instability, though he warned about collusion himself.","Critics reject the labour theory of value and point to the economic record of centrally planned states."],"context":["Enlightenment Scotland in the 1770s, writing against mercantilist trade policy and chartered monopolies.","Industrialising Britain and Europe in the mid-nineteenth century, amid harsh factory conditions."],"relevance":["Supply chains, competition policy and the role of trust in exchange still draw on his questions.","Debates on automation, the labour share and concentrated market power echo themes he raised."]}},{"id":"pigou-coase","a":"pigou","b":null,"bLabel":"Ronald Coase","title":"Pigou and Coase","frame":"Two approaches to externalities: correcting them with taxes and subsidies, or comparing bargaining, legal rules and government action once transaction costs are taken into account.","rows":{"question":["When private and social costs diverge, how can government move output to the socially optimal level?","When one party’s activity harms another, can the parties reach an efficient outcome through bargaining?"],"assumptions":["Government can measure the external cost well enough to set a corrective tax or subsidy, although Pigou himself warned that public authorities may lack the knowledge or independence to do so.","Harm is reciprocal, and transaction costs are usually significant, so the zero-cost bargaining case is a benchmark rather than a description of most externalities."],"mechanism":["A tax equal to the marginal external cost raises the private cost to the social cost.","Affected parties trade rights; with zero transaction costs, the efficient outcome follows whoever holds the rights; with positive costs, how rights are assigned matters."],"policy":["Taxes on activities with negative externalities and subsidies for those with positive externalities.","Clear property rights and attention to transaction costs; government action compared case by case."],"criticisms":["Critics stress the information needed to set the right tax and the political pressures on tax setting.","Critics argue the analysis neglects how the initial assignment of rights affects distribution and fairness; the difficulty of bargaining among many parties was a point Coase himself stressed."],"context":["Developed at Cambridge in the early twentieth century, building on Marshall’s welfare analysis.","Set out in The Problem of Social Cost (1960), partly as a critique of the Pigouvian approach."],"relevance":["Underlies carbon taxes, congestion charges and taxes on demerit goods.","Informs tradable permit schemes and legal approaches to nuisance, pollution and property rights."]}},{"id":"sen-income","a":"sen","b":null,"bLabel":"Income-based approaches","title":"Capabilities and income measures","frame":"Whether development is best measured by income or GDP per capita, or by what people are actually able to do and be.","rows":{"question":["What real freedoms and opportunities do people have to live lives they value?","How much output and income does an economy produce per person, and how fast is it growing?"],"assumptions":["People convert income into well-being at different rates, depending on health, gender, disability and environment.","Income is a useful general measure of command over goods and services, and of the means to well-being."],"mechanism":["Development expands capabilities such as health, education and political freedom, which are valued in themselves.","Rising income per head allows higher consumption and funds health, education and public services."],"policy":["Investment in health, education, social protection and democratic participation alongside growth.","Policies that raise productivity, investment and output, with income growth as the main target."],"criticisms":["Critics argue capabilities are hard to select, weight and measure, and that composite indices simplify the approach.","Critics argue averages hide inequality and omit non-market activity, health, freedom and environmental damage."],"context":["Developed from the late 1970s onwards, informed by Sen’s study of famines and welfare economics.","National income accounting developed in the 1930s and 1940s and became the standard development benchmark."],"relevance":["Underlies the Human Development Index and multidimensional poverty measures.","GDP per capita remains the most widely used single indicator for comparing living standards across countries."]}}]},"hash":"d2c7379a34fcdbe4"};
